ppetite came back, I put on flesh. it would do for us. Not only were we to have paper felt cheerful, ate well, and digested my food. My spirits clothing, but it was said that the time was not far were good, and my nerves strengthened. I continued distant when we should have such things as furniture, with the pills, until I put aside my sticks, able to walk railway carriages, and even-houses, all made entirely of easily and erect as of old. livery trace of rheumatism paper. These promises have not all been reached, but had disappeared. Now lam perfectly free from illness we shall bave the greater patience to wait for such and pain." things,if we can be suppl ied with dinner table requisites Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People make good, a so cheaply as to be able to dispense with that most dis- rich blood and fortify the nerves ; they have repeatedly agreeable of domestic duties. viz.. " washing up." cured Anemia, Indigestion, Palpitations, Rheumatism, Flowers as Sweets.—Candied violets, and rose petals ;Sciatica, Neuralgia, St. Vitus' Dance, Eczema, Nervous now figure among the sweets prepared for the tea table Disorders, Paralysis, Locornotor Ataxy, and Effects of but one would think that most people would prefer to Overwork or Worry, also Ladies' Ailments. Of all enjoy the beauty and fragrance of such flowers to eating dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., them. One might go further and say that it does not Holborn-vieduct, London, post free 2s. 9d. for one box, necessarily follow that because a flower is pretty and ,or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, sweet-smelling, it is good eating, and there are many nerfumed blossoms which are certainly not innocuous. By some it is supposed that violet leaves,but not violets I are a cuie for cancer, and it is said that damask rose leaves have certain medicinal properties, hut on the whole, candied flowers do not suggest the idea of an acceptable" bonne bouche." Women Cyclists.—ldeal weather for the time of year generally favoured the Easter holidays, the only draw- back being the terribly dusty condition of the country, and this state of things, and the increasing number of motorists, appear to be rapidly driving the woman cyclist off the road. It was very noticeable that the number of lady riders was much smaller than in former years, and this falling off is attributed to the objection- able dust, and the danger from the motor cars. Often when a motor rushed past, it was impossible to see any- thing for a time, and, unlike the motorist, the cyclist cannot protect herself from the dust by gauze veilings, or other devices, on account of theirbeat. One's cloth- ing cannot be adapted to meet such conditions, which involve so much cleaning, and hair washing, that many girls have more or less given up bicycling. Easter Attire.—Chnrch parade on Easter Day was a gay spectacle, for although many women, owing to the keen wind, retained their fnrs, the toilettes were all very spring-like, and light in hue. A few reds—straw- berry generally—were in evidence, but in the vivid sunshine these irritated and hurt the vision, and it was a relief to turn to the pale grey greens, and starch, and water blues, that promise to have an immense vogue during the approaching summer. A few apparently hardy individuals had thought the occasion a fitting one on which to sally forth in blouses through which theirarms and necks were plainly visible, and if they have succeeded in escaping pneumonia, influenza, or some other uilment of the kind, they should consider themselves more fortunate than the cat with its proverbial nine lives. Elbow Sleeves and Long Gloves.—The short, heavily pleated check skirt, and little taffeta silk bolero, may be useful, but they have become too popular for the woman who aspires to some little individuality and exclusiveness in her attire. There is no doubt what- ever, that we have made wonderful strides during the past few years in the art of dress, but we have yet much to learn from our Parisian sisters. who would not dream of purchasing ready-made skirts and coets( duplicates</p>
