      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TRACED HT Hls CLOCS.</head>
        <p>TRACED HT Hls CLOCS.</p>
        <p>At Warrington on Saturday, Henry Ellison was charged with stealing from the works of the Whitecross Company, some time between Tuesday, April 10th, and Thursday. April 12th, a quantity of brass tubing and a brass water gauge tap, of the value of £l.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Hesp said he was on duty in Lovely-lane at about 10.30 on Thursday night, when he saw the prisoner running away, and shortly afterwards found him lying on the railway embankment face down- wards. The brass was protruding from his pockeks and witness arrested him. In company with Detective Meacock witness followed prisoner's clog marks from whero he was found to a boiler et Whitecross Works.— Prisoner was sent to gaol for two months with hard labour, and thA Beech complimented P.C. Hesp on his smartness in capturing the prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LOGIC OF POLICEMEN.</head>
        <p>LOGIC OF POLICEMEN.</p>
        <p>" It would be a terrible thing, if we were held respon sible for the logic of a policeman," remarked 11r. Light- foot, a Carlisle solicitor, at Wigton, on Tuesday. " Policemen," he added, •' are the most illogical set of men on the face of the earth."</p>
      </div>
