      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>IMPORTANCE OF FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>THE IMPORTANCE OF FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Woman Chancellor</p>
        <p>of the</p>
        <p>Exchequer</p>
        <p>All housewives are Chancellors. They have not so much money to spend as the Nation's Chancellor and that makes their- job More difficult. They must &amp;r.re pennies and half-pennies where they can...</p>
        <p>Fels-NaDtha soap the light way—no boilincr—will save them some moneyi each week. And, don't forget that clothes when washed "the Fels-N.aptha way" are cleaner, 'whiter and better looking.</p>
        <p>Used</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha used 4‘ the Fels-Naptha way" does it.</p>
        <p>FELS-NAPTHA 39 Wilson Strcet London EC</p>
        <p>A Birmingham curate was to have married a working- class couple on Saturday afternoon, but at dinner time a message reached him that the couple would prefer to be married on Sunday, the reason given being that the bridegroom wanted to go to the football match ! LESS OAKUM.</p>
        <p>With the -view of reducing the amount of oakum picking in English prisons, the prison directors have authorised governora of prisons to give more local work not of efompetitive character, to prisoners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>C UEL TEMPTATION.</head>
        <p>C UEL TEMPTATION.</p>
        <p>Florence liodgkinson, Author of " TINSEL AND GOLD," Eic., Eic4</p>
        <p>HAPTER XXXY.-</p>
        <p>WHEN :Helen came to herself shet. was quick to perceive that her husband ha..; not the least suspicion of her secret.. Mr. Stuart was tenderness itself to his young wife; he never dreamt of Helen's fraud, his first theory on reading the newspapor ac- count being that the Miss Jardine he had known on board the " Cygnet " hul been saved from the wreck at the elevez-th bout and that she was the heiress referred ,o' in tne paper. ( A little reflection gave him an ev m more feasible idea, and one that he certaizly liked better, namely, that someone, knowing the story of the shipwreck and the Vast fortune which awaited Miss Jardine in Eng'and, had taken advantage of her relations nt•,ier having se,en her to personate the dead girl and claim Alandale in her stead. This last view of the case he communicated to Helen the following day, tiOng her to write at once to England ; as he pointed out, ,it was alike a duty to congralulate*, her friend upon her marvellous rescue Iron' the " Cyg- net " and to assist in unmasking an .imposter, therefore whether her letter reached the redl Miss Jardine or a false pretender it would do its work. • _ . _ .</p>
        <p>But [Help had had time for thought, too, and her conclusion' was that the girl now reigning at Alandale was -her sometime friend and companion, Hildred Jasper. To do Helen justice she was very kind- hearted, and now, instead of being filled with anger. and indignation at the fraud which she believed to have been -committed, she was full of pity for the sinner, for she felt that . the crime was the direct • outcome of her own deceit and that she had only herself to thank. " I see it all," mused the young wife. " Hildred believed me (lead, she thought that I who had been eager to share all I had with her in life would not grudge her my name and fortune when I needed them no longer, and she knew my only feelings for my relations at Alandale were diskrust.and aversion. _ .</p>
        <p>" I can see it all. My death had left poor Hiliked stranded, she had given up.her teach- ing and had no money to return to Melbourne, even if Miss Murray could have restored her post at the college to her ; she told me again and again she had no friends in England, sc her situation must have been simply terrible , she would shrink from appealing to the Alan. dale people, besides everyone round her would address her as Miss Jardine, and it would be far easier to accept the position than attempt explanation. Helen thought over things till she brought on a severe nervous headache, but her thought was not unavailing, for she had solved the difficulty of her next step. Not for worlds would she have used harsh measures to poor Hihired, but still Helen's views about Alandale had changed greatly since her marriage, for she knew that many of her husband's friends in England thought he had made. a misalliance, and if she could prove herself to have been Miss Jardine, of Alandale, their opinion would change by ,magic. Then, too, she hoped some day to have children, and though Willowdene was a fair estate and a handsome provision for, one son, yet Alandale would be a priceless heritage for another; in fact, before sha saw the newspaper Helen had nearly decided to tell the truth to her hus- band ; it simply hastened the end. She wrote very gently and tenderly to her girlhood's friend saying that, from the ac- counts she read in the paper, she could only believe that, thinking her dead, Hildred had suffered the exchange of theirs names to con- tinue.</p>
        <p>She told of her marriage and of her desire tc reclaim her inheritance, but she assured Hil- dred she was perfectly willing to take all ex• planifions on herself and finally suggested that her frienq should come to Italy, either to Genoa or some other town where they were both unknown, then she and her husband would come to the place directed and talk oyez things with Hildred tojsee how an arrange- ment could be made which, while prOclaimiru the truth, would yet screen Hildred as mud- as possible from blame. It was a kind and affectionate letter, and Helen directed it to " Miss Hildred Jasper," secure that it would be handed to the false Miss Jardine, since she would be thought thE only person at Alandale likely to know the friends of her late companion. For a whole three weeks Helen waited in suspense for a reply, and on being questioned by Ronald she declared that she had written and no answer had come. Mr. Stuart was more convinced than ever that Miss Jardine was an imposter and urged that he and his wife should start for Alandale .to denounce her, when the Unouement came. Mr. Stuart was :away on a shooting expedition and not expected home till dusk, when, on one of the last of the fair September days, Lord Alan- dale's card was brought to his .wife. " Show the gentleman in," Helen said to the English servant they had engaged on her marriage as a sort of maid-housekeeper. "He is a relation of mine, and as we have much • to discuss tell anyone •else who calls .that I am en- gaged." Kenneth started as he came into the presence of Mrs. Stuart. His long search had resulted in the discovery that a " Miss Jas- per " had been rescued. from the wreck of the Cygnet,"'and that she was married to one of her late fellow passengers; he had felt pretty sure this lady was the writer of the letter which had driven his darling away, but it was only when he saw her face to face that he felt positive, for then he saw at once that Helen was a Jardine to her finger tips, she carried her pedigree on her face. • ; I am quite sure that Y'ou are my cousin." "I ate Aubrey Jardine's daughter. Lord Mandale, you must deal mercifully with the 'girl whc took my place; the 'deception at first was nib)... not hers ; then later, When it was of untold talyantagel to her for the mistake to continue, she yielded to a great temptation. " I loved Hildred Jasper as a sister, and if she had robbed me for years instead pf months; I -should) say the same ; she must suffer as little as possible." " Mrs. Stuart," said peor Alandale, "I love your friend as my own soul. I don't care what her name is so ,that I may one day say ' she is my wife." " Tell me everything," pleaded Helen. " I ; wrote to Hildred hut she never answered my letter." ; Lord Alandale asked nothing better than to. tell Hilcued's story from the day she came to . England, and Helen's tears fell thick and fast during- the recital. " Holt she suffered ! Of course I always knew the.e was a great sorrow in her past, but I never guessed she was. married. • But whe. killed ht wretched husband after all ?</p>
        <p>"An Australian called Charles Gresham. He dirti before the confession was published. Mrs. wart, he asserted that ,he was engaged to you</p>
        <p>" is only partly true. I had known him ever since I was fifteen and he worried me into a promise to ' try being engaged to him.' I told 'aim I did not care for him, but he would not take 'no ' for an answer. Finally, we agrctd to be engaged for six months; after that, if I found my feeling for him did not ripen into love, I was to be free The day aftei making this compact, when Charley had already started on a long voyage, I heard that I was mistress of Alandale "</p>
        <p>• And you broke witti Grotharn on iI spot ? "</p>
        <p>." Yes, but not because of the fortune. Ti. moment after I had given him my promi.e repented. I wrote to him at once." And Mr.-Stuart was on board the a- ttet '</p>
        <p>" Yes, and he is the one love of my life. 1- • has no idea I am Helen Jardine, and if h, won't forgive my deception I think my heall will break." But Ronald Stuarit " forgave" his if. knowing that if she had deceived Jilin. at least she had not done so from any worldly motive. He told Lord Alandale he would gladly lei he resign all claim to the Jardine estate, but thc earl was peremptory in his refusal.</p>
        <p>" Mr. -Stuart, se have already had con- fusion enough," he said ; " all I want now is for the rightful 'heiress of Alandale to come home, but-"</p>
        <p>" You want the false heiress to get off scot free, is that it ? Well, "knew Miss Jc.ver on board the ' Cygnet' and I am posithe that only a cruel temptation would have male her act as she did."</p>
        <p>" Mr. Stuart, my clearest wish is to inarry her." Stuart whistled.</p>
        <p>" Isn't that well-nigh impossible, swing your home is at Alandale where eyer..,m.‘ must know of her deception It is true that my homestead is clost, to Alandale Hall, and that hitherto I have acy:d as manager of the Jardine e3tate. But a singular thing happened last week. Afrurl of mine, who was most indignant at the cruvl. suspicions thrown at me, died suddenly •;-,.1v1 by a codicil to her will added the yery day I was committed for trial left me twenty thousand pounds. If I can find my darlily and persuade her to' be, my, wife, this /110:10)* will find us a home beyond the sea."</p>
        <p>Mr. Stuart looked lost in thought for a I'm minutes, then he said :</p>
        <p>" I see only one thing for it. We must put the matter into a lawyer's hand 3 to see that all necessary forms are carried out. All the world need know is that the girl people he- lieved to be Miss Jardine confessed she is imposter and that my wife is Aubrey Jardial:'s real daughter.</p>
        <p>" Then after a little time a paragraph might be inserted in the papers announcing th 2 dev.tll of Hildred jasper, who for some Lionths regarded as heiress of Alandale. " Then • yoll• patl . will be C , lear. Yon must. marry the lady in her real name as 'Eldred Blake, widow, and since I have no dol:bt you always call her Helen the narao of Hilllrcd kcan fall into disuse.</p>
        <p>_ " If you are abroad for several years, de- pend upon it you can safely • return eventually • to -Alandale without anyone suspecting vfier countess is the girl they once met as niss Jardine." "we shall never return to Erngland," replied Kenneth. " Mondale is too full of painful associations for us. Air. Stuart, - you have been very generous, you have shown me the easiest way of shielding My darling from all cruel slander, but there's one boon beyond even you, you can't tell me where to find her," • There was a mist befpre Ronald Stuart's eyes, strong man though' he was, as he ans- wered : " I would stake my forthne that you do find her. Believe me, Lord Alandale, love guch as yours cannot fail in the search. " And I will predict something else, that your marriage will be a really happy one be- cause you have come. together against such fearful odds. Circumstances • may makJ it necessary for your wife and mine not to 'meet for some time, but, believe—me, Helen' and I shall never have one unkind thought of her."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXII.</p>
        <p>THE law which is said to more sci slowly tciok a new !departure for once, and what was called the " Jardine Succession Case" was settled in an amazingly short time. Joe Fielding managed things much on the lines hinted at by Mr. Stuart, with the result that as little blame as possible was thrown uron Hildred Jasper.. In very truth the majority of people round Alandale thought more kindly of the girl than they had ever done before.</p>
        <p>The certificate of her marriage with D:ck Blake found in the murdered man's possession came back to people's memory now, and such an intense dislike to the scoundrel was felt many. that they professed themselves able tt, understand his wife's attempting any schemt- to escape from being discovered by hila. It was easier far to forgive the 'wet urnli meelings which ,had so scandalised th-. neighbourhood now that people knew the twk„ .who had made the appointments were mat: and wife. - Perhaps Maria Mackenzie expressed ti general opinion when she tcild 'Mrs. Si ucr; soon after the latter's arrival at Alandale :</p>
        <p>, " We all of is picked holes in Miss jard::l, —as we called her—and yet we all liked in our hearts, and now we know what martyrdom her life was there's not one of;, u• but what would be glad and proud E.O I friends with her if only she would conic back." - Helen Stuart's eyes filled with tears, and t, Maria's amazement the beautiful, youn; stooped and kissed her. The Mackenzies were in Society rt,-,w and, somewhat to the surprise of the iron master who had spent years fighting plotting for that end, it came about at l. without any signal effort of his own. John Leigh rode over to Rockmount September morning and was closeted the master of the house.</p>
        <p>Sir John said simply that he loved nr. with all his heart and wanted her for</p>
        <p>His. private fortune was so ample tli.tt</p>
        <p>could' Settle fifty thousand_ ponds •On</p>
        <p>bride ; the property was entailed and it. go to his.eldest son; failing heirs of hi 3 ~\ reverted to a cousin, but there v,-,,51,1 plenty in any case for his widow. " Whoever takes Maria takes a liar. thousand pounds with her," said .\C; " I'd rather my girl married you, :4 than a peer of the realm because 1 (I,) you care for her .and never; gave a th;•u:;,,t ; her fortune."</p>
        <p>" My aunt will present • Maria at .the' drawing-room after Easter," said Sir cheerfully," and if you will let us be inarr• November we can spend the winter Maria says she should like to see the and I am fond of the South myself."</p>
        <p>• Peter Mackeilzie gasped. " You don't mean that you li?,,vesetil everything ? " " We've settled that we care for each ,u 1 Maria declares she will -not marry me uni..o, you consent, but f hope you are not play the part of stern father." Mackenzie wrung his future son-in-law's hand.</p>
        <p>"I'm quite content, and her mother 'wil; be delighted. She was always alraid Muria would be married for her money." Of course, the neighbourhood could not ignore the future Lady Leigh, and SO from that hour the Mackenzies were reckoned among the lite. As for Lady Mary Jardine, she approved of the real Helen far more than she had done of the false, but she kneu, that young married people are best alone, and so she removed from the Hall and rented Woodlands of Lord Alandaltt who was leaving Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>Few people knew the real cause o Kenneth's departure, but it was generally supposed that' as Mr. Stuart was fully quali- fied to manage his wife's estate the Earl's services as agent were no longer required. lie took with: him the good-will and l'egrets of the whole neighbourhood, and—a heart that was nearly broken. For all attempts to find his darling had failed, although he had actually, traced her to the little home in Byron Road and heard all that Mrs. Smith could tell him.</p>
        <p>'She went out after breakfast to get her luggage and buy a few things, and I'm sure she never meant not to return. I waited up till eleven that night. I spoke to the police even, and asked if there had been an accident to a young lady anywhere in the neighbour- hood, but I could hear nothing of her, and I believe, myself, she's dead." It was just what poor Alandale was begin- ning to believe himself, for if the poor girl still lived he must surely have traced her, and yet the explanation was very simple after all and jut as s,q. Meeting with Edwin Blake, her husband's brother and evil genius, Hildred had given him-half her little store of money to ensure at 'least her temporary freedom from his pursuit. But knowing he would trace her to Mrs. Smith's she decided not to return there, but fetched her box of newly purchased possessions and left it g Brixton Station, whence she took the train to Victoria, meaning to apply to a large registry office near there for a post a,s lady's maid. She happened to have in her pocket a reference she had written for her own maid, and this, she thought, might satisfy enquiries for her character. She walked on and, on aimlessly, hopelessly, till just what might have been expected happened; in her dazed state she did not notice the approach of a motor-car; she was knocked down and dragged by a policeman at the risk of his life out of reach of the wheels.</p>
        <p>They took her to a hospital and for weeks she lingered between life and death, Olen at last she was pronounced out of danger and began to wonder what would become of her `when turned from the shelter of the hospital. Among the ladies who visited the women's wards was a lady called Mrs. -Durant. She had silvery hair and a sweet motherly face, and so Hildred did not shrink away from her questions as she had done from those of others, with the result that Mrs. Durant soon learned she was a widow, homeless, friendless, and Well-nigh destitute. „ Mrs. Duiant. had a larger heart than many women; she invited .Hildred to come to her home on leaving the hospital and remain three months ;by that, time her health would be perfectly, re-established and her friend in need would try and find her a situation.</p>
        <p>"You xru2s't, not think you will be useless to me in the interval. I have a very Urge correspondence, for I am interested in a good many charitable societies, and you- may be able to help' me with my letters.' Hildred promised the help gladly, but, as it happened, she did not spend three months at Lancaster Gate.</p>
        <p>Just a fortnight after Helen reached Mrs. Durant's house, an Old friend of the latter's calling to see her on some charitable business asked to leave a message with her secretary, and this friend, Lord Alandale, was ushered into Helen's presence. " I want to leave a 'message," he began, then. he looked at her awl the whole expression of his face changed. Rushing forward he clasped her in his arms.</p>
        <p>"'Helen " " No," she smiled sadly, " never Helen any more. I was only an impostor." . "You are not an impostor, you are my life's love. Dearest, I have sought you wildly, despairingly, it cannot he that you will send me away now that I have found you." " I must—it would be wrong to listen." " Unless you marry me, dear, my life will not be Worth living. Oh, surely, surely you can't think I only cared for the 'heiress of Mondale ! I don't care what your name is, I can forgive you being Blake's widow, I want you yourself to be soul of my soul, love of my.heart."</p>
        <p>He went on to tell her of Helen's marriage and how she reigned with her husband 'at Alandale ; Mrs. Charles Jardine, he went on to say, had died suddenly, and Magdalen had entered an English Sisterhood as Probationer, while Lady Maly had taken Woodlands off his hands.</p>
        <p>"For I could not bear to be so near Man- date when it no longer held my darling. In any case I am going abroad for years. My passage is taken• in. the' ". Gordon," which sails for South Africa next week. Dearest, iiome with me and snake my exile bliss." And Hildred had not the courage to push the cup of happiness from her lips ; she loved Lord Alandale with all her heart and soul, so the graceful dark head rested on his shoulder and his lips sought hers again and again. He was an old friend of. Mrs. Durant's and ; he eaee the kind old lady the outline of his love story. " I cannot live without her, but she has scra.ples about , accepting me'; says it will rum' my future. Mrs. Durant, won't you make her realise I'd give up the whole world gladly for her sake ? " " I think with love like yours you must .mi.rry, and she's a rare creature, but, my boy, the marriage will to an extent shadow your life: Thaa-e is no sin but what brings its own nunishment, and it seems to me, Kenneth, ihat .your poor sweetheart's , deception will Ttoil a life long exile from your native land." It won't ha so- bad as that ; we can keep (-tear of Alandale,- and-7-people forget." Mrs. Durant thought the peerage would .remind them, bat though in due 'course that volume . announced the fact that Lord Alandale had married Mrs. Blake, the good people near his early home did not seem to .connect that name with that or the mur- dered 1--in's widow, the despoiler of Helen Jardine: But then, of course, they had been taught to believe 'that the pretended heiress was dead and Blake is a common name after all. " Till death do you part," solemn words, but no wedded pair had ever heard them with more (lecp joc, and thankfulness . than Alandale and his bride, for Ili* love . had been tried in the furnace and purified like refined gold. • ,</p>
        <p>Later, on the very day those words were spoken, Lord and Lady Alandale stood together on the quarter deck of the" Gordon," watching the ever-widening streak/of 'water which separated them from the mainland. " You are giving up home and country for me, Kenneth," breathed his wife. " God grant you may never repent the sacrifice."</p>
        <p>- "It is no sacrifice, dearest, for my home is where you are and my kingdom •is your heart.</p>
        <p>"We can lead a happy useful life in the fax South, my sweetheart, and if, years hence, we get a yearnink on us • for a sight of No old country and come back to it, I don't believe anyone will remember all that happened this summer and our share in it." .</p>
        <p>Five years 'have passed, the Alandales have not returned to England, but • Sir John and Lady Leigh, Nyho have visited them in fair Natal, bear testimony that theirs is an ideal union and .that the .beautiful Countess is the daring of her husband's heart and widely popular in her Southern home. Thew friends • speak, too, in warm praiSe of Viscount Jardine, a princely boy of four, ;Ind two tiny girls, Lady Hope and Lady Honor.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Stuart droops her eyes when she hears of these little ones that her husband may not see her unshed tears, for there are no little footsteps at Alandale hall and no childish voice calls Helen mother. It may well be in the distant future that the title and estate of the !Jardine family will be once more united and there.nmy come a day when the gentle Couatess returns as lawful mistress of the gran A old home where she ruled long ago for a fe,* brief months when she came from over the sea and yielded to " A Cruel Temp- tation."</p>
        <p>TUE. END</p>
