      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Election of Officers</head>
        <p>Election of Officers</p>
        <p>The fortnightly 'meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians WS held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present—Messrs. C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw,H.Eyes, G. Egerton Warburton, A. Broome, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies. J. Smith,T.Wright, S. Woodward, W. Harrison, G. Pilling. S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, J. Spruce W. M. Morris, P. Collins, T. Wood, J. Donalea, it. R. Norman, J. Rutter, a. Haßowe. .T. Whitley, R. Billington, C. E. Linaker, Inn.. W. Weir. G. F. Ashton (clerk). W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), Cook,Pearson, and de Prez (relieving "cers) and J. Cunnah (workhouse master.) ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN. „al? first business was the appointment of chairman att. Norman's re-election was moved by Mr. Morris n. 7" seconded by Mr. Douglas. An amendment- WEE roved for the apnointment of Mr. Baxter as chairman. s Pointed out that Mr. Norman had only served- 'a Years as chairman of the Board and the amendment "as withdrawn, Mr. Baxter being re-appointed vice- chairmin. 14r. Baxter in returning thanks said he thought his 1 friends had made a mistake in not agreeing to the anPointment of Mr. Norman. ELECTION OF COMMITTEES. Mcasra. Baxter. Woodward, Bolshaw, Chadwick, Harrison, .Tones. C. E. Linakor, Smith, Norman, Pilling, TeYlor and Wright were re-elected to form the Assess- ment Committee. The Finance Committee, were re- with the addition of Mr. Wood as follows : Messrs. Woodward, Wright, Chadwick, Douglas, A. G. ~Guest„"f, L. Jones, C E. Linaker.Roylance, Spruce and Teruperlev. The Ladies' Visiting Committee were npointed and thanked for their past services. The committee was constituted as follows :—TAndy Brook, r, Iss Dutton, Mesdames Armitage, .T. B. Chadwick, H. anareve Edelsten, Posnett, S. Taylor, Whitley, and W. Fletcher. it In Proposine the vote of thanks Mr. Woodward said ans a most difficult matter to get ladies to attend the , ntk of the committee and the naihes proposed had h°ne their work in some measure and he did not see taw they could be replaced. FREE MEALS. ,TheShipton-on-Stow Union wrote asking the Board snDport the following resolution : That the Board ss with alarm the Education (provision of meals) now before Parliament, first as the State would be • over in part the primary duty of parents and rat 4ly as an enormous cost would be thrown on the th eblyers. That a copy of the resolution be sent to liTembea.of Parliament representing the district of Union. the Association of Poor Law Union, and to each Union in England and Wales." It was derided to support the resolution. THE TREATMENT OF VAGRANTS. The Clerk to the St. Thm Union,Exeter, wrote askine the Board to supportoNe following resolution : —" That this Board vews with approval the exhaustive ninon of the Dep.n. rtntentalCommitteeon. Vagrancy, and urges opon the Government the advombility of taking action without loss of t• upon the, lines sug- gested by the Committee." time The Chairman thought the question of dealing with vagrancy was a very serious matter. It was decided to support the resolution. FINANCIAL. Mr. Woodward reported payments in nut-relief of ,S7O to Mr. Cook ; £26 to Mr. de Prez ; and £25 to Mr. Pearson. During the past quarter the vaccination accounts had totalled ,197 Is. 4d., a decrease of £1 115.3 d. as compared with the previous quarter. The mainten- ance of lunatics amounted to £436 3s. in the quarter as ajlainat £412 53. Gd. for the corresponding quarter of laSt year.</p>
        <p>TILE BOARD AND MR. WOOD. Mr. Spruce called attention to an article which had 'tiPPeared in a certain paper on the boarding-out ques- t'il°o- As the article reflected bpon the Board he ~.°ll y ght the ought to ask for an explanation from the "titer. - .34r. Wood asked if Mr. Spruce referred to an article Tgeed " T.W." Although he did not hold himself Ponsible to the Board for any opinion which he 42ressed as a ratepayer, he said nothing which might ct upon the Board. ir. Hallows thought the article reflected upon the "R)cers of the Board as Bell as the Board. 011 the motion of Mr. Woodward it was decided not entertain the %question. or tr. Wood denied that he bad reflected upon the Board Upon its officers.</p>
        <p>TALLEST ARMY OFFICER RETIRES. Major Oswald H. Ames, who has served nearly Lw,enty-two years in the 2nd Life Guards, and is tho "West officer in the Army, is retiring from the service. SNOW IN SCOTLAND. There were heavy showers of snow and sleet in Perth- lire on Tuesday. The Grampians and Ochil Hills were lickly coated with snow down to their base, and even ne of the low grounds were quite covered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 21, 1906. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 21, 1906. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>COLOUR IN EYEGLASSES.</p>
        <p>Before the French Academy of Medicine, Dr. Motais, of Angers, has read an interesting paper on the use of glasses as an aid to sight. Writing as the result of fifteen years of experiment and observation, the doctor condemns the use of blue or of smoked glass. He declares a yellow glass to be in all respects more suitable. It imparts to all objects tones that are warm and agreeable to the eye, and has an influence at once illu- minating and soothing upon the weakest and most sensitiic organs. This double, and appa- rently contradictory action upon the eye, Dr. Motais attributes to yellow glasses eliminating all ohemicfd rays from the solar spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>AN UNKIND FREAK OF FORTUNE.</head>
        <p>AN UNKIND FREAK OF FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>It is curious, says Cagliari Popular .Scienee, to note that the discovery of the X-rays very nearly came to Sir William Crookes himself, and it was an unkind and certainly undeserved freak of fortune that he just missed it. One day, when he had some photographic plates near the appa- ratus, he found upon developing them that they bore certain marks which corresponded with his fingers. Thinking that these were blemishes due to faulty manufacture, he sent the plates back to the makers with a few very strong, r4marksf He had taken X-ray pictures without knowing it. The first •X-ray pictures of the hand were very imperfect, the shadow being ill-defined, and showing merely the rough outline of the mem- ber. They were what a photographer would call "" out of focus.",</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NEW SWEET COMPOUND.</head>
        <p>A NEW SWEET COMPOUND.</p>
        <p>A new compound described by Di. T. Cigli has appeared in the , European chemical trade which is designed to imitate saccharine. It is known, says the Scientific American, as "banana essence." The taite of this syrup liquid is at first caustic and then bitter, but soon after very sweet. Its specific gravity is 1183 at 20deg. C., and it gives in acid reaction. Analysis shewa it to contain 54 per cent, of saccharine in combina- tion with a base analogous of pyridine. heated on platinum foil it gives white fumes, then burns with a bright flame, leaving a thin layer of carbon. When the latter is burnt the ash is negligible. The syrup give.; a precipitate with Nessler's liquid, and most of the alkaloid re- agents. Adding dilute mineral acids we can separate the saccharine as a white crystalline precipitate, and ether dissolves it again. By evaporating the ether solution we have whito crystals which melt about 225 deg. C. The author tried to prepare a solution of saccharine in pyridine, but did not obtain a product identical with tho above.</p>
        <p>THE MECHANICAL THEORY OF HEAT. Suppose that the descent of a heavy weight is made to impart motion to a revolving fan con- tained in a vessel of water. The motion of the fan through the liquid is necessarily accompanied by friction, which has the effect of warming the water, and, by a careful measurement of the exaltation in its temperature, , the quantity of heat produced can be measured. -In this experi- ment, says "The Story of the Sun," there are, no doubt, opportunities for the escape of heat, such, for instance, as in the, cooling due to the radiation from the vessel. It is, however, in- variably found that when all necessary precau- tions have been observed, and when due allow- ance has been mado for all losses, the figures invariably shape themselves towards that final and most important result which asserts that each unit of heat is precisely equivalent to the performance of 772 units of work; or that, con- versely, when 772 units of work are entirely de- voted to the production of heat, the quantity that they will generate is precisely one unit. The significance of this conclusion is appreciable throughout the whole range of physical science. Its applications to astronomy are especially is- teresting and it.structive, and its development o* the grandest scale is presented in solar phenomena.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MAKING ARTIFICIAL ALCOHOL.</head>
        <p>MAKING ARTIFICIAL ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>An interesting method of making artificial alcohol is described in the Zeitsehrift fiir Elektroehemie. When calcium carbide is acted upon by water acetylene is produced. This is the well-known method of manufacturing this gas. On the other hand, when aluminium car- bide is acted on in a similar way another hydrocarbon, marsh gas, or methane, is evolved. Other metallic carbides give rise to other hydro- carbons on the addition of water. And at St. Albans, in France, the carbide of a certain metal is manufactured from which ethylene is given off when water is poured over it. This gas is pumped into sulphuric acid, and ethyl hydrogen sulphate is formed. Water is then added, which produces alcohol and sulphurio acid. On distilling this mixture the alcohol passes over, leaving the acid, which can be used over again. The metal is converted into an oxide, which can be used in preparing fresh car- bide.</p>
        <p>ihis is not, of course, the first time that alcohol has been produced artificially, comments the Globe. Olifiant gas or ethylene can be made directly from carbon and hydrogen. Shaken up with strong sulphuric acid, it -combines with On the addition of water alcohol and sulphuric acid are formed as above, and the former is &amp;epee rated by distillation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ACETYLENE v. ELECTRIC LIGIIT.</head>
        <p>ACETYLENE v. ELECTRIC LIGIIT.</p>
        <p>The comparative advantages of acetylene and electric light are given as follows in the Build- ing World: Acetylene is usually superior to electric light for country houses, the light itself being the most pleasing of the artificial lights that are known. The oxygen that is consumed and the products of combustion are so small as to be negligible; that is. the hygienic qualities of acetylene are practically as good as those of electric light, and are very greatly superior to coal-gas or oil lamps. The cost is equal to coal- gas (naked flame) at 3s. 6d. per I,oooft. This is actual practical cost, allowing for every possi- bility, including reasonable .waste, and the coal. gas would have to be good to work to this com- parison. The cheapness of acetylene is largely aided by the small cost of the labour required, a farm hand or gardener's assistant doing, in about fifteen minutes daily, all that is needed. With electricity, supposing the power is ob- tained free from a' waterfall, the attendance of a skilled man is still required, as a labourer or outdoor servant cannot run an electric plant. If the attendant is paid El per week, the cost of the light from this cau,so alone comes high. Acetylene is quite as safe as electric) light; acci- dents are possible with both if foolish &amp;nits are done, but not otherwise. The days of doubt and difficulty as regards acetylene have now passed away, and a fully-guaranteed installation can be easily erected.</p>
        <p>• MALADIES _CAUSED BY THE AIR. To say that, bodily health and ketivity are con. ditioned by the quality of the atmosphero intro- duced through the lungs into the blood is a trite</p>
        <p>remark, but it is none the kss useful to study the extent of the application of this doctrine, says the Hospital. Possibly the general attention is mainly directed to the grosser contaminations edged with a narrow kilting of old-rose silk. These straps of lace are crossed over in the front duo to the introduction of poisonous fumes, as well as at the back, the small opening at the smoke, and other by-products of industrial se- tivity into the atmosphere, though the general neck being filled in with a yoke and collar of indignation also recognises, More or less fitfully, finer lace. The sleeves are full at the shoulders, r but are drawn in . closely above the elbow by however, the inconveniences which ari from means of small tucks, and finished with -two frills the imperfect ventilation of public robins and of lace. The hat that completes this costume is conveyances. The degree of danger in an im- of cream straw, bound at the edge with black perfectly renewed atmosphere in human habita- tions is not to ho measured m'erely, or even velvet, and trimmed with loops of velvet and shaded pink roses, mainly, by an increased percentage of carbonic DUST COATS. acid gas. Inimical as this undoubtedly is, there are yet in air that has been breathed substances At the risk of being thought premature, says much more prejudicial to health and vigour, and a writer in the Taller, I have already been giv- it is to these organic poisons that the ill results ing that most, useful garment, the dust cloak, of imperfect ventilation arc mainly due. The some thought and attention. .It is only within direct effects of these substances are bad enough tho last—well, never mind how many—years that in all conscience. hut their indirect consequences the dust cloak, which bears much the same rela- i are still more disastrous. It is, to mention but tionship "to the mackintosh that the parasol does 1 a single instance, impure air that paves the way to the umbrella. has developed from an exc