      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>- --- Many of our readers will be pleased to leara tha Mr. Thomas Edward Knowles, the son of Mr. J. H Knowles. 20, Lovely-lane, Warrington, successfully passed the Solicitors' Final Examination held in March last. Mr. Knowles served his articles with Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A., LLB., Solicitor, Egypt-street Warrington, In the .Excise branch of the Inland Revenue service Mr. L. G. Thomas, clerk, Liverpool, has been trans- ferred to Warrington, and Mr. S. Fry, clerk, Warring- ton, has been transferred to Liverpool. Athletic Successes. •</p>
        <p>At the Burnley Cricket Club's thirty-sixth athletic festival which was held on Good Friday, J. Houghton, Winnington Park, won the lap prize in the bicycle race. Time, 12min. 52sec. At New Brighton Tower on Monday. C. Berry, Warrington, arrived home third in the lap cycle race.</p>
        <p>Everybody should see our SEX GUINEA CYCLE; unrivalled value, J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55 Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Theft of Choice Fowls. Between eight and nine o'clock on Saturday night, Mr. E. C. Cooper, of 296, Manchester-road, had nine choice pure bred Minoreit and White Leghorn fowls stolen from a wooden building in his yard, this making the second robbery of the kind during the past 16 months. A crowbar had been used to break off the ironwork of the liencote. Information was at once given to the police, but no clue had been obtained up to the time of writing.</p>
        <p>Warrington Choir's Success. The Warrington Male Voice Choir are to be con- gratulated upon their splendid success at the Chestr Crown Eisteddf, d, on Easter Monday, when they carried off a prize of £2O, and a silvea crown for the conductor. The test piece was "The Spartan Heroes," a composition of Prothero. The contest was a very close one, the Warringion choir gaining the verdict by only two points. At the evening meeting of the eisteddfod the deputy conductor of the choir (Mr. Hassell) was crowned by Mrs. Mond, wife of the member for Chester, who had presented the crown for competition. Southport Vocal Union were placed second.</p>
        <p>J, ft. WOOD, Piano Dealer and Tuner, 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Adat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Sunday School Prize Distribution.</head>
        <p>Sunday School Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>_ On good Friday the scholars of the Logh-street _Primitive Methodist Sunday School held their annual tea and prize distribution. A large number of adults and children sat down to an excellent tea. after which a short musical programme was provided, the pro- gramme being arranged by Mr. Spruce, while during intervals the prizes (numbering about forty) were dis- tributed by Mr. H. Billingham. At the clos a vote of thanks was proposed by Mr. Bistle, the superin- tendent of the school, to all who had contributed towards the enjoyment of the evening, and this was acknowledged in the usual way.</p>
        <p>A Sunday Nuisance at Sankey. The district,adjoining the canal bank at Sankey, near Warrington, has long been the resort on Sundays of gangs of men, who assemble to gamble. Frequent complaints had been made to the police, but it was a matter of difficulty to effect a capture. Last Sunday week strange police officers were imported, and a successful coup was made. Two of the officers succeeded in mixing, with a crowd of about twenty men, and after watching for a time captured eight of the men, the others making their escape.—At the Warrington Petty Sessions' on Wednesday, Joseph Kirkham, Ralph *Edwards, Albert Atkinson, George Muir, Edward Dyke, Alfred Jacklien, David Roscoe, and James Jaye, all residing in the neighbourhood, were fined is. and costs for gambling, and also 2s. 6d, for being obscene language.</p>
        <p>PREMIER CYCLES are hard to beat, 7,8, 10, 12 and 14 guineas. Cash or twelve monthly payments. Sole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge at., War- rington.—Advt. Warrington . Sailing Club.. . .</p>
        <p>A boat race, under the auspices of the Warrington Sailing Club, took place on the Mersey on Saturday afternoon. The course was from Widnes, round a buoy moored near Walton Arches and back again to the west end of Thewlis's wall. There were nine entries, antl all turned out: The race was an excel- lent one, and produced a very close finish. The first four home were :—Pride (fimm.), captain, W. Warbur- ton ; Gipsy (51min.), captain, J Devison ; Emmie (23min.), captain, J. G leave; and Rosie Mary (s,ratch), captain, J. Leigh. The arrangements were admirably carried out by Messrs. R. Leigh (starter), G. Hough- ton (judge), H. Warburton (commodore), J. Devison (vice-conamodore), R. Looker (treasurer), and W. H. Warburton (secretary).</p>
        <p>LEA-FRANCIS CYCLES—Britain's Best. Prics reduced, Demand increased. Inspection invited. Sole agent :—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., War- rington.—Advt. Warrington P.S.A. Society. There was a good attendance at an open meeting of the Warrington Society which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. Mr. E. Wynne Jones delivered an address on the " Resurrection of Jesus Christ." He stated that the resurrection Was one of the great foundation stones of the Christian religion. It was second only in importance to the Crucifixion. If Christ did not rise then delusion had been better than knowledge, error infinitely grander than truth. The fact 4 Christ's resurrection rested upon evidence which could not be explained away. The plain unvarnished story which the Gospel writers told could not be overthrown. Let them cling firmly to that truth for the .truth of Christianity did not depend on a set of texts, or doctrines, or creeds, but on a mighty fact. Mr. Alfred Bate rendered two violin solos in a very creditable manner. Mr. B. Wadsworth read the Scripture lesson. Mr. W. Brown also took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>Warringtonlan's Death In Australia. News has just been received here of the death at Maryborough, Queensland, Australia, of a member of an old Warrington family in the person of Mr. George Caldwell, eldest son of the late Mr. William Caldwell, builder, Warrington. The deceased in his early days I carried on business in the building trade at Warring- ton, but subsequently left for Wigan, and after a short I period of residence there he set sail for Australia in , 1862 The following intimation of his death appeared in "The Wide Bay and Burnett News" of March 6th: Still another of Maryborough's old identities has passed away, Mr. George Caldwell having died on Saturdyy last at his residence, Walker-street, at the advanced age of seventy-two years. Hr. Caldwell has been a resident of the town for somewhere about thirty-sir years, having arrived here in 1870, coming from Brisbane where he landed in 1862. He followed his trade as a monumental naalson and contractor, having carried out the erection of many of our principal brick buildings, tic. He had been in failing health for some time, so that his death was not unexpected. Mr. Caldwell leaves a widow and two daughters to mourn their loss. The funeral took place on Saturday afternoon and was largely attended.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCISHIRE:AdVt. Death of a Warrington Bandsman.</p>
        <p>The death is reported of Mr. Tryal Holcroft, late of Algernon-street, after a few weeks' illness, at the age of 43 yews. Deceased was a prominent bandsman and was for .some years a member of the late Mr. T. J. Down's Band, and of the Earlestown Prize Band (London and North-Western Railway), the Warrington Borough Prize Band and the Cheshire .Lines Prize Band, ' Liverpool. A. capable musician and an expert player his services were in great demand and he had played at the Crystal Palace, Bello Vue, Nov! Brighton, Rhyl, Colwyn and Clough Hall. The funeral took place, at the Warrington Cemetery on Sunday, and marked sympathy was shown by the bandsmen in the district. The members of Mr. Down's Band walkedln front of a massed band com- prising the Earlestown Prize Band, the Warrington Borough Band, the Cheshire Lines Band, Liver- pool, the Pioneer Prize Band, Messrs. Oros- field's Prize Band, ,the League of the Cross Band, and the band of the Warrington Volunteers, altogether numbering about 80. The coffin was carried shoulder high from the house to the Cemetery by bandsmen in uniform chosen from each band in attendance, the massed band playing the Dead March in "Saul." The hearse followed containing the wreaths and the instrument played by the deceased His widow and children,relatives and numerous friends followed in carriages. The funeral service was con- ducted by the Rev. T. Al J. Ridpath (St. George's Mission). Many floral tributes were placed on the grave including a beautiful artificial wreath from the !members of the Earlestown Prize Band. The musical arrangements were carried out by Mr, Fred Molyneux and Mr. Peers Brimelow, two intimate musical friends of the deceased, while the funeral arrangements were , entrusted to Mr. G. Platt, Howley-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>'•• • - •• I', :,.., • • **"-</p>
        <p>..</p>
        <p>...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM •</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM •</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>NEW FANCIES FOR BRIDESMAIDS.</p>
        <p>In these dap; of novelties, fashions for wed. dings are ever changing, the latest, according to Mrs. Hugh Adams in London Opinion, being bridesmaids' hats trimmed with real flowers and small fruit. Already an enterprising Parisian florist has been exhibiting small toques made en. tirely of real orange blossom and adorned with a cluster of leaves and gclden fruit for wearing at weddings; while a London firm have on show big picture hats, fashioned of grass-green chip-straw. adorned with real roses, orchids, lilies, or lilac. The Spanish marriage will un- doubtedly influence many weddings this year Already the favourite gift for bridesmaids is a fan designed after the antique Spanish speci- men with engraved ivory sticks given by Queen Maria Christina. of Spain as a wedding present to Princess Margaret of Sweden. NEW SKIRT MODELS.</p>
        <p>The new spring skirts are truly fascinating and smart in the extreme. Checks, tartans, and fancy stripes are general favourites in woollen goods, and nothing could look fresher and more spring like than these taking varieties of green and white, brown, blue, grey, and black-and-white shepherd's plaids. Of the two new models shewn in our illustration the first is a corselet skirt, fashioned in a deep cream flannel, having lines of purple in two shades running through it. The material is cut on the cross towards the centre front, the double pleats opening over a straight ra nal of the same material. A pretty finish is</p>
        <p>given to the waist by the way in which the outer pleat is cut to lie over the inner one, tab. fashion, the tiny pearl buttons giving just the touch that is needed. The second is a taking model in French grey-and-white plaid tweed. The skirt is also cut with a gored seam' towards the front. This style, of cut gives a particularly smart lonk-waisted appearance to the figure, and also tends to make the wearer look very slim. The skirt fits closely round the hips, and is ar- ranged with double box-pleats down back and front. These are flatly stitched down nearly to the, hem in front, and not quite so far at the back, while the hem is very wide, and falls in graceful flutes.</p>
