      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MARRIED WOMEN'S DEBTS. –</head>
        <p>MARRIED WOMEN'S DEBTS. -</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the wide currency which ha* been given to the recent decision of the House of Lords in the Paquin case, it may be doubted, says the Queen, whether the present position of a married woman as regards debts contracted by her is generally understood. It maybe useful to explain that the effect of that decision is this, that if a wife living with her husband orders goods, with his authority, although this is not made, known to the traaesman supplying her. who may have dealt with her on the assumption that she was ordering the goods on her own per- sonal credit, yet the tradesman can only look to the husband for payment, and, if it should turn out that he is not worth powder and shot, the tradesman is without any remedy, even though the wife may have ample separate property. Moreover, even if a tradesman supplies goods to a married woman, knowing that she has no authority to pledge her husband's credit, or that she has been, in fact, forbidden to do so, yet. al- though he can sue-her, he can only obtain pay- ment out of any separate property she may have which is not tied up so that she cannot anticipate or change it; he cannot make her a bankrupt unless she is carrying on a business apart from her husband, nor can he obtain a Committal order against her. Truly a curious state of things. Small wonder that the tradesmen are agi- tating might and main for an alteration in the law, which is admittedly anomalous, having re- gard to the changed status of married women.</p>
        <p>UP-TO-DATE GOWN. A smart gown of old-rose-coloured voile is shewn in the sketch. The corselet skirt is finished at the hem with three wide tucks, and arranged in stitched pleats about the waist. The bodice is very full, and pouches slightly over the high-waisted skirt. It has two tucks running horizontally round the figure, whilst over the ohoulders there are straps of thick guipure lace,</p>
        <p>I - ' 4 Y 07....„ ii f t:,t,;4•1,:. ",..,. CI.LIa6. c V .1 ik : 1 4. ii \ , 11 ' t 1 ') l' , ~ , -3$ - „ ‘ 14t, At 1. ; \ \ \ - 1100 -.., q , ,r - +i,lia, t• \ ..„...._ t 1,..,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>AND IT CAME TO PASS.</p>
        <p>[BY EMMY') OSBORNE.]</p>
        <p>"The great thing in this world is not in much where we stand as in what direction we are moving."—OLlvea WENDELL HOLMES.</p>
        <p>And it came to pass in this wise. A certain young man one ,a 1 ay heard strange things at the mouth of one of the common people. It was on the evening of a Sabbath day. As the folks, who were then known as respectable law-abiding &amp;tizens, were wending their different ways to wait upon the same Gil, he went to liiten to one who spoke of strange things by the wayside. The speaker was not like unto those others. He did not clothe himself in garments of the same sombre hue neither did his face look so solemn ,and foreboding. His clothes were simple and his countenance unlike those others was the sun 3 as yesterday. His tie was red, which at that time was not considered to be fashionable by certain of the people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>THERE WAS A MAN.</head>
        <p>THERE WAS A MAN.</p>
        <p>The bells of the churches had stopped pealing. There was one bell, however, which tolled for a time to help those who had stayed to) long over their toilet to make hurry. That after a while ceased and all was quiet. One could imagine in the calm of that evening, as a small crowd gathered together, how many many years ago in a land far off certain things happened almost in the same way. There was a Man. He was called poor by those who knew Him not. He had a simple message for a homely people. Haying such a message, and baing in earnest he went not into the places of the rich—save to suffer—but spoke where all who would might hear it. There were many who scoffed at Him. That was as it is now. The man is gone : the message remains. Who amongst us is to take it up and bear the burden ? It is not light. There are many who prefer ease and comfort, to be smiled on by the rich and fawned on by some of the servile poor. Christ has yet to come into His own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>HIS MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>HIS MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>A message was given .at the street corner on that Sunday evening. It was delivered by one who perhaps believed not in God yet the words were of God. It ran thus : The tongue of the sucking child eleaveth to the roof of his mouth for thirst: the young children ask bread, and no man breaketh it unto them. A simple story was weaved together. The words .could be understanded of the people and the truth they could not gainsay. That which would give life unto the poor and wisdom unto the rich was unfolded so that none went away. The cloud lifted and the light was bright and radiant. The stone of hum initv which the builders rejected, the same now became the .head of the corner. At last there was a way out of the chaos of civilization. The people were to come into their own. Their heritage wig to he returned to them. Happiness was theirs, and as the soesksr finished the last rays of the sun peirced the sky and as we wended our ways homewards darkness came over us, To-morrow, was in our thoughts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>TO-MORROW.</head>
        <p>TO-MORROW.</p>
        <p>There have been many to-morrows since then. I have thought much of the message and I have worked hard to more clearly understand its meaning. Mine was not a sudden conversion. It may be that some can take you to the middle of a field and point oat the very spot where they received the light. It may be, too, that it would have been better for themselves and others near to them if the sudden light had appeared to them in' their homes. Their home-life would have been sweeter and happier, perchance. To-morrow! Why should we think of to-morrow. Why not live the life to-day and then all to-morrows would. be the same! How often have we heard sermons hnil speeches which have quickened us and have set us on fire. How often have we made up our minds that come what ntay we will begin to set our house in order to-morrow. And then on the morrow we have mixed up with the world and the great ideals of social reform have taken unto themselves wings and have fbwm away. They have, may be, returned many times but each succeeding visit has been of shorter duration owing to our varied excuses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>IT MAY BE.</head>
        <p>IT MAY BE.</p>
        <p>Some months ago I visited after an absence of nearly twenty years the place of my birth. I remem- bered it as a charming little village. Green fields surrounded by quaint stone walls, interspirsed with , little low-lying cottages. There were not many folks ' there. Each body knew the other. They shared in each other's joys and sympathized in one another's sorrows. To me it was an ideal spot. But what change had come over the place. I could riot retell' ' any of its charms. They were all gone. Stay. Had , they all disappeared ? Had the place changed or had I? Probably, if I had not been away f rorn the villlage, had not seen other places, had not made other friends, , the little village would still have been my paradise. And it may be if I had not heard that serm )n on Socialism that my old thoughts of life would have remained unchanged, and they would have still been dear to me. Who knows ? • A CEUNGE DESIREE).</p>
        <p>Since then I have talked with many Socialists T have read .arnd thotwht much. I have realised that property has its duties as vell as its rights and that Christians, who believe in their God, and have a desire to serve, may make the lives of those around them happier and sweeter by their presence. I have tried to understand the feelings of the oppressed poor. have wondered wh,ther a system which allows so many people to live on the yo-ge of starvation whilst there is so much wealth in and on the land is a good one. I have thought of the cares and anxieties of the .poor mother who has had not the bread to feed her poor ill-clad, ill fed children. I have seen the man bowed down, knowing that he was strong enough to work but could not get it. I have heard the little child cooing in the arms of the sorrowing mother, tears rolling down her cheeks the while. And I have seen riches flaunting themselves. Idle landowners whose pigs are better housed and better fed and better thought of than multitudes of human beings. I have been with workmen employed in reafing pheasants for our landowners and have heard them speak of the cost. I have been engaged in flagged School work and in l'3l.C.A.'s, in work social and in political, and have striven to do some good according to my ability but it has all come to this. That in future, I do not send my time in attempting to lop off branches of the diseased tree. I am with the Socialists. I am with the party which is working. hard to kill an unfair system, M make the lives of the people—the common people if you like—brighter and happier, to bring back the land to the people, to pro- duce for use and not for profit, and thus strive for a fair and eqnitable return for man's labour. The day of the hanger-on, the parasite is fast drawing to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>COMES TO PASS.</head>
        <p>COMES TO PASS.</p>
        <p>One bubts whether real reform can ever cow from political parties which have no desire to assist more than they can possibly help to uplift the masses of the people,and when one realises that the pheasants are of as much interest to Liberal as well as to the Tory landowners and that we can be duned with our eyes open by Promises which are never fulfilled,it may be that the call has not come iu vain. j believe with Edwin Markham when he sings ; •</p>
        <p>All that we glory in was ones a dream; The world-well marches onward, gleam by gleam. New voices speak, dead paths begin to stir ; Man is emerging from the sepulchre ! Let no man dare To write on times great way. "No thoroughfare." And it came to pass. To-morrow has come at last. A new day with new hopes and great desires. A future full of promise A flouse of Commons where perorations, daily and rightly, may be followed by some practical measures. The starving women_ of Paris broke into the first national assembly of the great French Revolution with the cry of " Bread ! Bread ! not so many long speeches." The women of England, too, are rising. They are sick unto death Their lives are being shortened and their children maimed. They are joining in the fight for freedom. The time for action has come anctall those who desire ,to see a noble race must act with a will and a heart, In the name of common sense don't trifle with it. Trifling is simply playing into the hands of those individuals who have private interest to serve, who have no desire to help only in return for compound interest at extortionate rates, and who will keep on the back of the workman so long as he is contented to carry therm</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SON CHARGED WITH MURDER.</head>
        <p>SON CHARGED WITH MURDER.</p>
        <p>Benjamin Nicholls, a brickyard labourer, was si IVolverhampton charged with the murder of his in other, Mary \Blackham, 53. It is alleged that the prisoner, whilst drunk, hit and kicked his mother, breaking five ribs and rupturing the spleen.</p>
