      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Musical Service.</p>
        <p>A musical service was held in St. Peter's Church. on Sunday afternoon and was well attended. The speaker was the curate, the Rev. D. R. James. An excellent programme was submitted, the vocalists being Miss Marie Houghton, Mrs. Hannan. and Nlr. Gittens, who were ably assisted by the Apollo Male Voice Quartet. Mr. P. Webster was at the organ. Bold-street WesleyanlChureb. There were large congregations at the Easter Day services at this church. The Rev. ,T. Manning preached in the morning, his subject being a defence of the Resurrection. At the evening service the Rev. S. Pitt occupied the rostrum and preached on the words : "And as they thus spake, Jesus himself stood in the midst of I them, and saith unto them, Peace be unto you " (Luke xxiv., 3(i.—The services were of an appropriately joyous character, Easter hymns being sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. S. Pitt preached at this Church on Raster Sunday morning on the words : " Totteh me not, for I am not yet ascended unto the Father" (John :VC. 17). At the evening service the Rev. .1. Manning was the preacher, his text being : " And, behold, two of them went that same d-ty to a village called Ernmaus, which was from Jerusalem about threescore furlongs. And they talked together of all these things which had happened. And it came to pass that, while they com- muned together and reasoned, Jeans Himself drew ne.tr and went with them. (Luke xxiv. 13 to 15.) There were large congregations. On llonday a large party of the Wesley Guild con- nected with this Church indulged in a "ramble" to Helsby via terodsham !nd spent a very pleasant afternoon in that delightful neighbourhood. About 40 sat down to tea, and the picnic proved a great success, the weather being beautifully fine.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. , There was a fair attendance and a good meeting of this Society on Sunday. Mr. Aut presided and spoke upon " Prove all things. As one who had investigated the side of irreligion, he had found its sweets soon turned bitter, and left an aching void. On the other hand he had proved that being a follower of Christ, it gave out a sweet experience and lasting joy. Mr. H. Hanson, of Manchester, spoke upon "The resurrection of Christ." To the Christian Easter was the most interesting season of the year. If Christ rose not then our faith was vain, but the testimony of Christ's life, death and resurrection was proved beyond all doubt, not only by the Biblical historical facts, but by historians notott all favourable to Christianity who bore testimony of the actual life, death and resurection of Christ. He (the speaker) was once a sceptic, but the light of the Gospel proved by history had fully persuaded him of the truth of the ' Gospel. Mr. Beswick and Mr. Healey took part in the meeting. Special hymns for the occasion were heartily joined in. The Secretary gave a cordial welcome to one new member making a total of 445 or three more than twelve months ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Church</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday when the preacher was the Re,.. J. F. Matthews. In the morning, taking for his text John XII., 24, he said sacrifice must precede the up-springing of the soul's hest and truest life. In the parable con- tained in the text Christ referred to Himself. He was the grain of wheat dropped in the uncongenial surround- ings of our common life. His living was rkart of His dying and His dying became a condition of human living. I His dying was a Divine decree and became the mystery of life and of living. " Christ loved me and gave Him- self for me " was the true Easter of the soul. For , himself he could not see that the up-raising of the body of Christ had much bearing npon ourspiritual existence. We were not saved by the resurrectionhut by the dying and the erected Cross. Christ said : "I am the resur- rection and the life. He that believeth in Me, though he die yet shall he live, and he that liveth and believeth in Me he shall never die." There must be a hiding away, a sustaining grace and divine life within tho soul. Life was hidden for a purpose—that it might be made manifest. His life for ours and our life for His might be seen through it. In the evening the Rev. J. F. Matthews took for his subject "New hope for the defeated.," pointing out that the poorest and most theadbare life was of infinite worth to the Father. All our social schemes however valuable were pointless if they did not bring about r. complete reformation of men</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Meeting.</head>
        <p>Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Meeting.</p>
        <p>The ennui]. Wesleyan foreign missionary meeting was held at Bold-street Church on Good Friday evening. Mr. James Dodds presided over a good attendance and was supported by the Revs. G. Lester (Bahamas), Cecil 11. Wright (British Guiana). S. Pitt (superintendent of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit), %V. Mellor and J. Manning.</p>
        <p>The ehAunt AN, who was received with applause, said he had in a quiet way always supported foreign missions ' hut he had not felt that nnboutfded enthusiaern and that Iburning zeal which ought to characterize a platform advocate. He knew that it was the last command of the Master that they should go into all the world and preach the gospel to every creature. Missionary work had only begun comparatively recently ; before the end of the 18th century hardly any work had been attempted and the present imposing magnitude had been gained during recent times. Few things had a higher moral grandeur than the beginnings of Christian missions during the last century. The work had not been such as to elate or discourge the friends of the mis- sions. There were 1,500 million people in the world of whom 400 millions were professedly Christian or a little more than a third, but he believed that ere long they would see such an outburst of Christianity throughout the world that would surprise those who were not acquainted with the work which had been ' carried on. (Applause). Allusion was made to the work in Japan and China, and the chairman expressed the pleasure it afforded him to know that theJap sailors now on a visit to this country attended divine service the other day. Like Japan, China was coming to the West to learn and he believed that the future of that great country would he in the Christianity, and the Prot ,stant Christianity, of the West. (Appluae). The Rev. W. .lEr LOR read the general and brutal missionary report. God's work he said, had been greatly blessed during the past year in the mission field, especially in India, where there had been a great spiritual harvest. Financially, the accounts were not yet to hand, but the income would be substantially the same as in 1901, namely, a little over £134,000, but the expenditure would be somewhat larger, so that the deficit of 1904 would not he diminished and might even` be increased. With regard to this circuit he would not say they had not done so well as in the previous year, although they had not raised quite as much rnoney.as he thought this was due to the fact that in the previous year they had a simultaneous canvass which resulted in the receipt of a number of donations. He hoped they would do better next year. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Places which showed a decrease were Bold-street, Bewsey-road, Sankey Bridges, Latchford, Ellesmere- street, Lymm, Whitley, Antrobus and Stockton Heath, and the net decrease was £24 14s. 9d. Penl:eth headed the local list with £52 Is. 11., and it well deserved the honour. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The collections from the various churches in the circuit were as follows :—Bold-street Church, £39 Os. 7d. ; 13ewsey- road, £3O 15s. 2d. ; Penketh, £52 Is. id. ; Padgste, £3B Gs. 3d.; Latchford, £ll Gs. 3d. ; Lymm, £125.125.2d; Sankey Bridges, £8 17s. 7d.; Ellesmere-street, £21.5s 3d; Stockton Heath, £4 3s. Dd. ; Whitley, £9 Bs. 11. ; Antrobus, £ll 4s. ; Appleton, £5 ss. 4d. • Nlertinscroft. £4 3s. 9d. ' • and Moore, £22 15s. 9d., making a total of £252 14s. lOd. (Applatre). Interesting addresses upon work and experiences in the mission field were delivered by the Revs. Cocil H. Wright and George Lester. In his address Mr. Wright referred to the cosniopolitan nature of the population in British Guiana. In the country were thousands of imported Indian coolies, and if they could only get hold of these and teach them the Gospel whilst they lived in a nominally Christian land, and then send them back to India it would be one way—and he did not know a more blessed way—of bringing that part of the British Empire to the feet of Christ, and India would become one of the brightest jewels in the, Master's crown. (Applause). The collections for the anniversary (including the previous Sunday's services) amounted to £26 Ss. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>AN AWFUL</head>
        <p>AN AWFUL</p>
        <p>SKIN DISEASE</p>
        <p>Sores Covered Neck and Cheeks— Itched Day and Night—Nothing Did Me Any Good—Was Growing Worse —immediately Relieved, and</p>
        <p>SPEEDILY CURED BY CUTIGURA REMEDIES</p>
        <p>Miss Nellie Vander Wiele, of Lake- side, N. Y., writing under date of April /8, 1904, says: "I do wish you would publish this letter in the newspapers, so that others suffering as I have may see it and be helped. I suffered for many months with an awful skin disease, sores covering my ears, neck, and cheeks. Scabs would form and they would swell, and itch day and night. Then they would break open and blood and matter run out. I had tried many different remedies, but none of them did, me any good.. I was growing worse when I tried the Cuticura Remedies. The first application helped me, and when I had used two cakes of Cuticura Soap, three boxes of Cuticura Ointment, and three bottles of Cuticura Resolvent, I was completely cured."</p>
