        <p>If present signs are any GERMANY IN indication of what he TANTRUMS. desired to obtain, the</p>
        <p>GERMAN EMPEROR has been 'much disappointed by the result of the Algeciras Conference. His recent telegram to COUNT VON GOLUCEEOWSKY was something of an enigma; but it is now in process of being ex- plained by the German Press. If WILLIAM IL were not of such high rank in Europe his telegram would have been . regarded as s rather vulgar impertinence. When a distinguished foreign order was offered tet one of QUEEN ErazzinaTn's Ambassadors that imperious lady instructed him to refuse, remarking that she preferred to tar her own sheep. The EMPEROR of AUSTRIA, we cannot help thinking, might have been left to congratulate his own Ambassador at Algeciras. The congratulations of the GultmAli EMPEROR were impertinent partly because they were so public and partly because they wore an intrusion; and thoy were vulgar because they pre- sumed that Austria had done a favour to Germany irrespective of the merits of the case. It now appears that the telegram was intended less as a compliment to COUNT VON GOLUCHOWSKY than as an admonition to Italy. The official Press—it was, by the way, the independent newspapers which were stigmatised by Prince 113tsmARcx as "the reptile press"—has dotted the GERMAN EMPEROR'S i's and crossed his t's. It tells Italy in quite unmistakable language that, as it did not support Germany without reserve at the Algeciras Conference, it must look out for unpleasant conse- quences. Italy was asked to instruct her repre- sentative, the most experienced of Italian states- men, to support Germany at any cost. The WIARouisIrimooNTIVERosTA declined to be</p>
        <p>instructed. He went into the Conference with an unfettered judgment, and in no way disposed to regard the worse as the better reason. The conga', quence is that Germany, which professed to 101 satisfied with whit had taken placa at Algecitas,i6 now in a minatory,:and even threatening mood trots', which it may be inferred not only that the Extrcao* Wimseia did not get what he wanted, but that b° , wanted a great deal more than he believed hiraolf , entitled to get. The incident is deeply instril°' tive in various ways. It throws light, in the fir 4 place, on the EMP4IIOIeB ideas of diplomacy. i I wished to win at Algeciras,not through the weighs i ,of his cause, but by the number of his pledge</p>
      </div>
