        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>adherents. The man who plays for money with loaded dice is universally execrated. The GERMAN</p>
        <p>EMPEEDE, it appears, sees no objection to loading the dice at a Conference of the Powers. He was intent merely on winning the game. Italy is now told, with that brutal frankness which 'PRINCE Bram inex introduced into the Giar- n-tan methods of diplomacy, that it would have done much better to have followed instructions than to have exercised any judgment of its own. It is reminded, with no excessive sacrifice to truth, that it owes its independence mainly to Girman andlthat it has more reason than an'.' other Party to the Triple Alliance to keep German 'friendship. The additional reason is, of conrs,i, that it is more open to attack than the other Parties to the Alliance. It is seldom that nations 1180 this kind of language to each other, except in the privacy of despatches. Indeed, the case may be said to be almost unprecedented. A definite revelation of the GPIRM kN E NIP E ROIL'S views is one of the consequences. Hi is deter- mined, it seems, as might, in fact, already have been surmised, to become the D'ctator of Central *Europe, and, if possible, of Europe at large. A line of action which indicates that Italy has a mind of its own is, therefore, exceedingly exisperatinct. AR alliance, to the G E E E non's way of thinking, does not imply consultation as to mutual interests ; it means that one of the allies -shall give orders to the rest. We cannot profess to regret that this difference between Germany and Italy should have arisen. It will bring home to the EMPEROR WILLLor the fact that he is not yet, at any rate, the European D'etator. TwO of iris apologists in this country have recently pub• lished books in eulogy of his character. They; describe him as a peace-loving monarch. Possibly be is deserving of this praise. Syne day, never- theless, be may shatter the pogo&amp; of Europe merely for the sake of a paltry predominance in small affairs.</p>
        <p>The outcry about spoliation THE SECULAR and the "sacredness of SOLUTION, trust deeds" still continues. We should think that the rase in the street is learning something if he is going aboat with his eyes and his ears open, It ,I 3 obvious that the view of the Anglican or Catholic In regard to education is a purely selfish one. He !vela% have Bible teaching pure and simple and he Is horrified at the idea of secular education. The three "R's" are worthless in his view unless a Strong dose of dogma can be administered at the same time. This outcry against ridding State schools of the religious instruction incubus can h,ave only one sequel if it continues. The Hon. aek• L. Stanley M.P. said this week that "Secular 'education was in the background and might be forced to the forefront, if the opponents of the Bill Were too strenuous and insistent. The people of the country might say: We have had enough Of religious squabbles and insist on the logically Perfect system.' " There is no doubt at all upon ,the point. The Labour party is almost united in 'ta demand for secular teaching, and the great Mass of the people would rather see education stand as a national charge and responsibility freed entirely from religious teaching than that the war of the sects should continue.</p>
        <p>It is well that the rate- ATTENDANCES OF payers sheuld know how GUARDIANS. their representatives on public bodies attend to their ;Illties. A return of the attendances of the mein- ears of the Warrington Board of Guardians and :Rural District Council has just been compiled and presents some interesting facts. Mr. George Mather possesses the highest record having attended 115 meetings (including committees). The Warrington Borough Guardians are,of course' not called upon to attend Rural District Council meetings, which are confined to the country Guardians in the Warrington district, so ' that Mr. A. J. Joiley's total of 106 is ' most creditable—being quite the best for the Board and committee meetings. Next in order comes Mrs. Clay with 98, and as the ladies are not members of the Assessment Committee these figures show that she has attended to her duties with great diligence. With regard to the other lady representatives Mrs. Wilkinson attended 87 meetings and Miss Broadbent 67. The poorest attendances were made by Mr. Joshua BAD, whose. total was 9, while Mr. J. W. Knowles, Was not much setter with 13.</p>
        <p>It is said that the reply of . a Londoner to the question, "Have you heard of the Olympic Games ? " was t•_" Have they a new show at Olympia ? " To the A.,' ocknev, ot course, the vast hall at Kensington, With its recreation ground, is more familiar than are the lonian Isles in the classic land of Greece. According to announcements made several weeks ago, our King and Queen, together with the Prince Rod Princess of Wales, should witness on Monday, at Corfu the Olympic Games. For many centuries these exhibitions have been famous amongst the Greeks, having been first instituted, it is said, in honour of Jupiter, 1453 B.c Every fifth year the celebrations took place on the ..„banks of the Alpheus, near Olympia. Their object was to encourage young men of the nation in five different kinds of combats. So much belauded were the victors that they were regarded as national heroes. ' Incidently, such trials of strength, necessitating intelligence and courage, 10. conduced ta the preservation of health of the ' community, and diverted the minds of the people. Primarily, however, sport in ancient Greece had an utilitarian object. It was not indulged in, as it is in our country to-day, in and out of season, merely to pass time agreeably.</p>
        <p>* * Si The 'minutes of the Education Committee to he pre- sented at the monthly meeting on Monday do not furnish much information with reference to the plogress of the Bolton School at Latchford. rodtinder has been received from the Local Govern- ment Board showing that they are alive to the delay, but the Sites and Buildings Committee has done nothing more —in the course of two meetings— than to consult with the architects and refer consideration of their sug,gsstions to a later meeting. Oct of all this thought and deliberation it is to be. hoped that some method of erecting the school at a Ifigure more nearly approaching £.1.0 per head than the original contract rejected by the Town Council, will be devised. Though the borough rates have decreased, the Education rate is higher and the call is still for economy.</p>
        <p>FARMER INJURED BY A MOTOR-CAR. ' On Monday a farmer named Rayner was driving out of North Walsham, when a motor-car, approaching from Sheringham, dashed into him. Rayner was thrown out, and was caught in the motor-car and seriously injured. His horse had to be shot.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>recovered from the bottom of the sbaft.—Nfackintosh's body was removed to the Clock Face Inn close by, and Mahon was taken to the St. Helens hospital, and attended by Dr. Reid. The inquest was held on Wednesday at the Clock Face Inn. Mr. IL Hall, Government Inspector, was present, and Mr. Hewlett, certified manager, presented plans. Alfred Roby, carpenter, of Leach-lane, Sutton, St. Helens, said that when they started he asked Macintosh if it would not be better to start from the bottom, and the answer he gave was," When I went to school my master always told me not to waste my master's time. We will start from here. It will save us time."—Mr. Hewlett said that Macintosh was the man in charge of the work and the shaft. They had put a great many ropes into that pit for the pumps, and they had all been uncoiled from the bottom.—The Coroner said it required no scientific training to show ' which was the best and West method. Mr. Hewlett, manager, said that deceased was a man Of superior intellect and studious habits. They were sorry to lose such a good man.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
