      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE RETIRE ME NT. P.C. Addison, supernmners ry constable at the collieries of Messrs. It Evans and Co., Ltd., Haydock, will retire from the constabulary on a pension at the and of this mom.h. He has been in the constabulary nearly 26 years, having been at Haydock 23 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EDUCATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A petition in favour of the continuance of definite religious teaching in elementary day schools has been signed by over a thousand people in Earlestown and Newton, and practically every householder in Win RICIC LARGE LOCOMOTIVE ORDERS FOR VULCAN FOUNDRY.</p>
        <p>It is authoritatively stated that the Vulcan Foundry, Limited, of Newton-le-Willows, I ancashire, who some few months ago were awarded contracts for no fewer than seventy-six six-wheeled coupled goods locomotives for the Oudh and Rohilkhund, the North-Western (India), and the Eastern Bengal State Railways, have now secured a further order for seventeen similar loco- motives for the Oudh and Rohilkhund Railway, for which system the Vulcan Foundry, Limited, have also been commissioned to build a crane locomotive of similar design to those lately delivered to the Great Central Railway and the Western Australian Govern- ment Railways. • NEWTON MAN'S THEFT.</p>
        <p>At Warrington County Petty Sessions, on Wednesday Ernest John Gibbs, a young man, residing in Newton, was charged with stealing a gun, value £5, from the Newton Railway Station on February 15, 1905. the property of the London and North-Western Railway Company. He pleaded guilty.—Prisoner was employed as booking clerk at Newton and it was his duty to receive and enter up parcels. The gun was sent from Birmingham and after it left Tamworth, where it was transferred from the Midland railway, all trace of it was lost. Inquiries were made and it was found that prisoner had stolen it.—The magistrates sent him to goal for one month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Appointment of Officers.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present : Messrs. C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, G. Egerton-Warburton, A. Breome, T. Baxter H. M. Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. 11. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, S. Woodward, W. Harrison, G. Pi I 1 ag, S. Horton J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, J. Spruce, J. Rutter, J. Whitley, J. Haflows, R. Billiugton, C. E. Linaker, Jun., W. Weir, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Rough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical officer.) APPOINTMENT OP tIfAIRMAN. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward was -nominated for the position of Chairman of the Council, but having held the position for three years, he declined the office and proposed Mr. Pilling. Mr. Woodward's motion was approved. Mr. S. Wright was appointed vice-chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>6611 f MITTEES.</head>
        <p>6611 f MITTEES.</p>
        <p>The parochial Committees for the various parishes were re-appointed. Messrs. Wright, Chadwick, Baster,Bolshaw, Chrimes, Eyes, Guest, Linaker, jun., Roylance, Guest, Smith, Spruce, Stokes, Piling and Woodward were appointed Finance Committee. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Messrs. Woodward, Wright, Billington, Bolshaw, Broome, Chadwick, A. J. Guest, Harrison, Guest, Jones, Linaker, Linaker jun., Mountield, Piling and Boylance. were re-appointed the Isolation Hospital Committee. The other Committees of the Board were also re-appointed as follows :—Parliamentary Committee : Messrs. Spruce, Bolshaw, Broome, Chadwick, H. M. Davies, C. E. Linaker, C. E. Linaker. jun., Roylance, Smith, Talbot, Whitley and Woodward. Isolation Hospital Committee: Messrs. Woodward, Wright, Billington, Bolshaw, Broome, Chadwick, A. J. Guest, Harrison, Jones, C. E. Linaker, C. E. Linaker Jun., and Roylance. Building Committee Messrs. C.E.Linaker, Antwis, Chadwick, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, Eyes, Guest, T. Guest, Hallows, Jones, Smith, Warburton, Whitley, und Woodward. Smallpox Hospital Committee : Messrs. Harrison, Jones, Browne, Roylance,Wrigh t, Pill i ng,and Woodward. Highways Executive Committee: Messrs. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, Antwis, Pilling, Baxter, Harrison, Jones and Whitley. Buildings Committee : All the members of the Council. The Chairman and Vice-Chairman were appointed ex-otilcio members of all committees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WALTON SEWAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>WALTON SEWAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Local Government Board had written sanctioning the application of the Council for power to borrow £1,620 for sewage works at Walton Inferior. The Board were advised that the existing sewer in Whitefield-road proposed to be retained should he carefully examined and if found unsatisfactory reconstructed. In any case manholes should be constructed at every change of gradient and directionand proper means of ventilation provided. WAHRINGTON'AND NORTIWICTI LIGHT RAILWAY. The Clerk read a letter from the Light Railway Com- missioners stating that the promoters of the Warrington and Northwich Light Railway had satisfied the Com- missioners that there was sufficient probability of their obtaining the necessary capital for the construction of the proposed light railway from Barnton village through Northwich, Great Budworth and Stretton, to Stockton Heath, and of that part of the undertaking being pro- ceeded with and completed. It further appeared that the promoters maintained the intention to complete the whole scheme by also constructing the line between 1 Barnton village and Stretton, and the Commissioners decided that extension of time asked for the whole undertaking should be granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>1 The annual vestry meeting was held in the Kingsley 1 Chapel of the Parish Church, the Rev. I-I. Blogg (vicar, rural dean) presiding over a good attendance. The accounts presented by Churchwarden Riley showed , receipts £370 12s. 9d. and expenditure £333 3s. 46. : leaving a balance in hand of £37 9s. 3d. At the "Iron i Church" there was a balance in hand of £6 Os. 3d., and at the Bridge Mission Church of £lO 2s. 10d. The accounts were regarded as highly, satisfactory and passed. The report of the Churchyard CO'lllllittee showed that . £92 9s. Bd. had been collected and £74 ss. 2cl. spent on limprovements, leaving £lB 4s. 6d, to be used for the present year. It was Stated £3O would be required this year for the same purpose.—The Chairman requested the parishioners not to place artifical flowers under the glass shades on the graves, but natural flowers.—Mr. Thomas Riley was re-appointed Vicar's warden,Mr. E. G. Steward parishioners wardeu,Messrs. J. E. Davies, John J. Wilkinson, F. ft. Kennerley, W. Burrows, J. H. Davies, and E. Sutton sidesrnen. Messrs. W. N. Hutchings and T. D. Timmins were elected lay representatives, and Mr. Timmins was appointed hon. ; ; auditor. The accounts of the various charities were ; submitted as required by law.—Mr. E. G. Steward, on ' behalf of the parishioners, presented the chairman with an Easter offering of £ll, for which the Chairman returned thanks in suitable terms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Crossley —Lewty.</head>
        <p>Crossley —Lewty.</p>
        <p>T LIE OLYMPIC GAM Es.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>---tr---</p>
        <p>The merrie month of Mity will not be ushered in with any organized celebrations at Warrington. The annual horse parade which originally constituted our May Day display will be hold on June 2nd—the likeli- hood of less fickle weather conditions having influ- enced the choice of date.</p>
        <p>• * * The Easter holiday is so often disapplinting from the weather standpoint that the ideal conditions which prevailed during last week-end were doubly welcome. Doliday makers had a splendid time and Warrington was almost deserted on Monday, the railway com- panies having to cope with a great traffic to the various favourite resorts. * * * The cyclone tore its horrid way across the land- scape: The air was filled with dust, trees, cattle, human beings and sections of scenery. In a moment it was all over. The sun peeped timidly through the murky atmosphere. Its light revealed the awful devastation of the storm. Just outside the danger zone stood a man. " Darn it ! "he ejaculated, " why didn't be blow his horn ! " * * * _</p>
        <p>Thursday was Primrose bay,. though there was not much evidence of it locally, beyond the usual wreath outside the Conservative Club. Last year an anony- mous Tory Free Trader deposited against the statute of Lord Beaconsfield in Parliarhent Square a great shield of primroses, on which was inscribed in violets Disraeli's famous dictum, '" Protection is not only dead, but damned."</p>
      </div>
