      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 21 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 21 1906.</p>
        <p>ST, HELENS.</p>
        <p>years; his father being treasurer at one time. His two brothers had been members, and his sister was still connected with the society. (Applause). During the last it; years he had had the great honour of conducting the rehearsals and concerts. He could assure them he felt extremely proud of the society, and 'of the position he held in it. What other society was , there in that or any other town which could bring together so many people in so many different walks of life? They met nearly every week unconcerned with social influences, sectarian differences or political worries for enjoyment of the practice of the divine art of music. Mr. Gilbert had said in " Pinafore," "Love levels all ranks," and ha (Mr. Crossley) thought they might add that music also did so, bed more effectively It had been truly said that " music is a glorious gift sent straight from heaven itself." (Applause.) tie assured them that the society occupied a great place in his affections, and it wasa pleasure to him to know that his efforts had given satisfaction. 'Mr. Crossley con- cluded by thanking the members on behalf of his fiancee and himself for their great kindness and the magnificent present. (Applause.) The CIIAIRM 4,N said he hoped Mr. Crossley would convey to his tlanc(ce their warm congratulations and best wishes. He would like again to express how much they appreciated Mr. Crossley's services. Those who were not now members of the choir could still appre- ciate the work he had done,and he (Mr. Monks) particu- larly wished to express his satisfaction at the way in which their conductor had got together such an excel- lent band. (Applause,) M. R. T. FA IRCLOUG Sr said he was one of the oldest members of the society, if not actually the oldest, as that completed-his 40th season. (Applause). He could remember the valuable work of Mr. Crossley's father in the office of treasurer. Since Mr. Crossley had been conductor they had gone forward and had never looked behind them. He heartily joined in the expression of good wishes towards Mr. Crossley. (Applause). -Mr. W. E. ATKIN said that as he bad been secretary of the society for a considerable number of years he should like to be allowed to ridi a few words of con- gratulation. Not only as conductor, hut in the matter of business Mr. Crossley's services had been an eminent help to the society. Had Mr. Crossley not been a ! musician he would have made a wonderful financier— ' (laughterand applause)—and that remark was proved by the present satisfactory condition of the society which was due in a great measure to Mr. Crossley's energy. (Applause). " For he's a jolly good fellow " was heartily sung, and a vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Burton —Gaskell.</head>
        <p>Burton —Gaskell.</p>
        <p>• There was a fashionable wedding at St. Lawrence's Church, Frodshatn, on Thursday, the contracting parties being Miss Mary Frances Gaskell, eldest daughter of Mr. Holbrook Gaskell, jun., of Erindale, Frodsham, and Mr. Charles Henry Barton, second son. of the late Mr. William Barton, of Liverpool. The bride is very well-known and highly esteemed in the district and great interest centered in .the ceremony. On all hands there were signs of rejoicing and in the church a crowded congregation assembled. The entrance to the church was adorned with the wish, " Health and prosperity," in huge red letters on a white sign, over which floated two Union Jacks—one from each side of the door —knotted securely together over the archway. The floral decorations in the church were confined to the chancel, which presented a perfect bower of greenery, with palms and tall ferns towering above a thick white border of spirma and azalea.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 11. B. Blogg, vicar of the parish, officiated and was assisted by the Rev. G. Corfield. The bridegroom was accompanied by his brother, Mr. Frank Burton, as best man. The stewards &amp;who. attended to the numerous guests in the churchNvere Dr. Burton, Mr. Holbrook Gaskell, and Mr Frank Bell, all wearing orchids as their badge of office. . The service was fully choral, the musical portion being under the direction of Mr.C.H.Flibbert,L.L.C.M., who played appropriate festal music including the. "Bridal March" from " Lohengrin " (Wagner), " Marchia Feitivita" (Macon), " Grand March in G " (Smart), and " Marche Triumphant " (Hibbert) ; and after the ceremony "The Wedding March" (Mendels- sohn) and several numbers from Ilendelssohn's " Lieder Ohne Worte." The service began with the hymn " :Praise the Lord, ye heavens adore Him," and concluded with "0 Perfect Love." The bells were rung before the ceremony, and rang a joyous peal as the wedded pair left the church.</p>
        <p>The bride: who was given away by her father, looked charming in a wedding robe of ivory duchesse satin, trimmed with lace and chiffon. The bodice was arranged with a transparent lace yoke, beautifully embroidered with seed pearls, and the short elbow sleeves were finished with Ince ruffles. There was a long Empire train, edged with soft frills of lace and chiffon, and the bridal veil of cream tulle was arranged over a tiara of orange blossoms, myrtle, and white heather. The bride's ornaments were a diamond and peridot pendant, and a gold and platinum strap bracelet, both of which were gifts from the bridegroom, and her lovely shower bouquet of lilies of the valley, white roses, and orchids, was tied with long white satin streamers.</p>
        <p>- - - Six bridesmaids were in attendance —the Hisses Dorothy and Christine Gaskell (sisters of the bride), Miss Helen Gaskell (c itsin of the bride), Miss Marjorie Mullinson, and two little girls, the Hisses Barbara Barton and Kathleen Gaskeil, the bride's sister. The four elder gi-ls wore dainty frocks of painted gauze, the skirts being thickly gauged at the waist, and finished at the foot with narrow frills. Folded fichus draped the shoulders below the lace yokes, and there were artistic touches of wallflower- coloured velvet on the bodices, which wpre finished with waistbelts fastened with rosettes o, the same • velvet. Their hats were of white clinoline and chiffon, wreathed with gypsophila, white daisies, and wallflowers. They each carried a basket of lilies of ;the valley and wallflowers, and wore turquoise matrix pendaits set in gold and gold chains, the gifts of the bridegroom. The little girls were charmingly attired in cream satin frocks made with -deep yokes and pointed lace collars, and, instead of hats they had wreit.ll4 of white mpss-roses. They carried small basket,; of lilies of the valley, and wore amethyst. heart-pendants on gold chains, the gifts of the bridegroom. A reception was afterwards held at the residence of the bride's parents, • which WliS attended by about. 200 guests. The honeymoon is being spent in Lynton, Devon- shire. The bride wore a charming travelling costume of periwinkle blue trimmed with velvet of a darker shade, and a crinoline hat with clusters of roses and knots of gold braid. The numerous presents were of la handsome and varied description.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The semi-finals in connection with the Warrington Works competition have been fixed for this (Saturday) afternoon, and should attract a good attendance to the Wilderspool enclosure. In the closing matches of the second round L. and N.W. defeated the C.L.C. by 111 points to nil, and Pletchers inflicted a crashing defeat upon the Wire Rope team, scoring 30 points with- out their lines being. crossed. The third round resulted as follows :—Glreening's, 13 points, Walker's, 11. points ; Fletcher's, 9, Whitecross, 0; L. and N.-W., 9, Corporation,7 ; Crosfleld's, 14, P. and K., 3. The first match to-day will commence at 3.30. and will be between Crostield's and Greening's, whilst Fletcher's and L. and N.-W. will meet an hour later.</p>
        <p>JOHN kV ALKER AND eo 11_,IIXAMINER" OFFICE,</p>
