      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ATTEMPTED TRAIN WRECKING.</head>
        <p>ATTEMPTED TRAIN WRECKING.</p>
        <p>Serious Outrage at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Warrington police and Inspector Ramage of the Cheshire Lines Committee are at present engaged attempting to solve the mystery of an attempt to wreck the London and Liverpool express on the Cheshire Lines Committee's railway at Warringtonlon Saturday.Up to the present however, they have no clue as to the identity of the perpetrators of the outrage. The express it appears, reached Warrington nt about nine o'clock in the evening, and the driver reported having run over an obstruction near Marshhouse-lane. No time was lost in isv esti gating the circumstances and it was found that twor railway sleepers, each weighing about one cwt., with four chains attached, making an aggregate weight of from 3001 b. to 4001 h., had been placed across the metals One of the sleepers had been cut in two by the train, while the other had been broken into com- paratively small pieces, and these had been scattered up and down the permanent way for several yards. The lifeguard of the engine—a heavy piece of metal weighing about a cwt—harl been broken off and it was found on the line. The train consisted of seven carriages, most of the occupants being holiday makers and bad it been thrown off the line, it must have been hurled down a steep and moderately large embank- ment with most serious consequences. Whethe,. the outrage is the serious work of some evil desposed persons or the thoughtless prank of boys is not known but in any case the work must have been expeditiously done as only ten minutes previously the Manchester express passed over the spot without encountering any obstruction. Interviewed on Tuesday, Mr. Derbyshire, station master at Warrington, said that Inspector Ramage, of the Cheshire Lines, Liverpool,had been making inquiries in the district, and the Warrington borough police had also taken the matter in band, but IT to the present unsuccessfully. The statement published in several morning papers that the permanent way was torn up for about 100 yards was incorrect, the track not being damaged at all. Neither were the sleepers which were placed across the rails fastened down, or the train in that casexvould have met with a serious accident. As it was, the sleepers taken from a disused stack close by were smashed and thrown at one side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GASTRIC CATARRH AND INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>GASTRIC CATARRH AND INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>The root of all physical we.tlcness is indigestion and exhausted nerve cells : when the stomach gives out innumerable troubles follow. A healthy stomach is our best investment Mrs. J. W. REACHER, 24. Frederick Road, Gorleston- on-Sea, was suffering from loss of flosh,chronic indiges- tion and nervous weakness, with severe headaches. Nothing appeared to do her good until she took VENO'S S MAW I:ED TONIC ; this famous remedy accomplished a complete cure. VFNO'S SE &amp;WEED ToNrc possesses marvellous strength- ening and healing properties. The most successful remedy for stomach, liver, kidney and blood diseases I especially successful in female weakness and constipa- tion. For purity, medicinal activity and all-round potency it has not its equal anywhere. Cures perma- nently. No return. Price is. lid., and 2s. 9d., at Chemists everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FINES £lO A MINUTE.</head>
        <p>FINES £lO A MINUTE.</p>
        <p>Ten men appeared in rapid succession in the dock at Worship-street at four o'clock on Tuesday afternoomand were fined £.5 each for" obstruction " by betting in the streets. The £5O was paid over within five minutes, the bookmakers, for whom the men acted as agents, having, apparently, quickly received intimation that their agents were captured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ELLEN TERRY JUBILEE.</head>
        <p>ELLEN TERRY JUBILEE.</p>
        <p>elptain 11. Graham, hon. secretary of the Ellen Terry .Tubilee Fund, has received a message from lqarkTwain, in which he pays the following tribnte to the great actress : " Age has not withered nor custom staled the the admiration and affection we have felt for you for so many, many years. I lay them at your honoured feet with tho strength and freshness of their youth upon them undiminished." The subscription list now totals :34.052 shillings. /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL AND SUNDAY OBSERVANCE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL AND SUNDAY OBSERVANCE.</p>
        <p>The Sunday observance question which has been prominent at_the annual meetings of the National Free -Church Council is to be the subject of a special con- ference at the Caxton Hall on Wednesday, May 9th. This National Conference is being called by a joint committee composed of leading Anglicans, Free Church- men, and Labour men. A letter has just been issued by Rev. J. Scott Lidgett M.A., President, and Rev. Thomes Law, General Secretary of the National Free Church Council to the General Committee of the National Council inviting them to co-operate at the Conference which will be presided over by the Arch- bishop of Canterbury. This Conference is to be the inauguration of a great national movement to safe- guard the Day of Rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PROOF, UPON PROOF. . This is a Statement made by a Warrington Woman</head>
        <p>PROOF, UPON PROOF. . This is a Statement made by a Warrington Woman</p>
        <p>Over 41 years after Mrs. Welch 27, Dolman's-lane Warrington, told about her cure by Doan's backache kidney pills, she said :—" It is all quite true about the good Doan's pills did me some long time back, and I have had no return of the old troubles. My cure has proved a lasting one,rind I am always glad to recommend the medicine that did so much for me." Here are the details of Mrs. Welch's case, given in a statement she made at the time of hef^cure :—" You are quite welcome to publish my testimonial for Doan's backache kidneys pills, and I hope it will be the means of others trying the medicine. "My ailment was kidney disorder, and the symptoms were bad pains in my back and loins, dimness of sight, giddiness, and urinary trouble. I was very low and feeble, and I had to take hold of the furniture when I moved about the house. Specks seemed to be floating before my eyea, and I felt full of wind. "I consulted a doctor, and he told me my kidneys were diseased, and I should have to be careful or dropsy would set in. He gave me some medicine, but I got no better, and as time went on I grew worse. "When I began with Dean's backache kidney pills I did not get much benefit from the first box, but I per- severed with the medicine, and with the second box I began to improve. Gradually the pains left me, my sicht became clearer, and the kidney secretions were natural. I have derived more relief from Doan's pills than from all the medicine I trigd. I shall do what I can to recommend the pills." '• Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street Oxford-street, London, W. Mrs. Welch was cured by Doan's; be sure that you get" Doan's."</p>
      </div>
