      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MAGAZINE REVIEWS, ETC.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE REVIEWS, ETC.</p>
        <p>PARIS GAIETY AND LONDON HUMOUR.</p>
        <p>Mention has been made of the gay and careless nature of the Parisian. Has anyone except a true-born Londoner over observed the humour and fun which lie in great masses among the people of London? Mr. W. W. Jacobs in modern days has depicted some of this in special particulars. Dickens, above all writers, most faithfully portrayed many phases of it. Thackeray has dealt with it in a manner not likely to be repeated. But all three authors do not collectively make up the mass of London humour. It is everywhere. It peeps out with drivers of public vehicles who use their horses as friends from whom to draw inspiration for their sallies of humour, and one wonders what will become of all this when the horseless vehicle is the universal mode of vehicular traction. Surely the man who turns a hand.e is not the same as the man who holds the reins, and cannot get out of electricity and petroleum what has been got out of the pulsations of horses. It comes to us from the railway porters and servants who keep at bay the troublesome multitude by deftly turning into broad farce events which begin seriously. It comes, too, from hotel and restaurant waiters, who see enough of the grim humours of life to become an almost endless source of inspiration. But it is also apparent on the surface. Butcher-boy and baker-boy and shop-boy are full of it. They carry their goods along in happy ignorance of the sport they give to those who can note the humorous in life. And the costermonger and itinerant dealer, to be met with almost everywhere, are special products of London who cannot fail to attract. One does not quite meet the counterparts of these people in Paris. Those who take their place there are not so distinctive, and partake more of the characteristics of the average Parisian. They send out, therefore, to the observer only what the average Parisian sends out, and do not stand apart as types of what the city can do in the way of currying on the humours of the time. Some day, perhaps, there will arise a greater humourist in London, who will , penetrate what London produces in this respect; and when this shall happen London will appear a happier and more genial place than is commonly supposed.— From "A New Tale of Two Cities," in the ' Cornhill Magazine" for April.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"FRY'S MAGAZINE" FOR APRTL.</head>
        <p>"FRY'S MAGAZINE" FOR APRTL.</p>
        <p>The April " Pry's" appears with a striking cover representing a rifleman firing straight out of the picture, and below is printed Lord Roberts's now famous phrase: " A Nation of Marksmen." The subject of rifle shoot- log, as the cover indicates, is treated at some length,and with great lucidity, by Mr. C. B. Fry, who draws atten- tion to the state of things existing in continental coun- tries at the present time, and points a comparison with England. Mr. Fry draws his chief moral from Switzer- land, where " every boy of fourteen can handle a rifle safely, and fire it safely with a stop-bott the size of a barn-door." Apropos of the same subject, Mr. B. .J. D. Newitt contributes a well-thought-out article on" The Sporting Possibilities of Rifle Shooting." The other features of the magazine are well up to the high average standard. J. H. Taylor and G. \V. Beldam provide golfers with some admirable hints, illustrated with special action-photographs, on `"The Open Stance." "The Forward : His Art "—alho illustrated with action- photographs—cannot fail to appeal to all players of Association football. " Danger Moments in Cycling " has most ingeniously conceived diagrams, showing the chief perils that beset cyclists and the best way to avoid them ; and " Borders Beautiful "comes peculiarly fittingly at this season for amateur gardeners. But perhaps the most interesting item in the number, next to those on rifle shooting above referred to, is another essay by Mr. P. A. Valle, the well-known New Zealand author, entitled" Things John Bull may learn from his Sons." Here Mr. Vaile denounces the management of the Lawn Tennis championships in England, besides giving a number of useful tips which might be noted with advantage in other fields of sport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PREHISTORIC TALES.</head>
        <p>PREHISTORIC TALES.</p>
        <p>The "Windsor gagazine " for April contains the second funny story in the new series of " Prehistoric Tales," most humorously illustrated by Cecil Aldin,frorn which we cull the following . . . _ _</p>
        <p>"fig and Zug had been very happily married for a year, when a member of the tribe, named Mug, Calll9 to reside in the empty cavern opposite. Mug appears to , have moved in early in the spring of 9999 u.c. The season was certainly springtime, but the prehistoric records are uncertain, within a thousand years or so,and ' the precise date cannot be accurately stated. " Moving was not a very arduous business in those days, and Mug, being newly rnarried,had little furniture i to shift beyond two pairs of reindeer skins and his out- fit of flint choppers and scrapers. "The bride simplified the move by wearing the whole of her trousseau ; but a civilized observer would hardly have noticed it. Zug, of course, did notice it. She was nursing her baby at the entrance of fig's cave, and, Icatching sight of Mrs. Mug's pebble necklace and bone • earrings, she instantly grew dissatisfied with her own Sunday-go-to-meeting skin-girdle. I "The Mugs' cavern was:situated some twenty yards inland of fig's cave, And Zug had sometimes proposed using it as a spare room for the accommodation of her husband's bachelor friends. For Zug was rather a prude, and, if the legends may be credited, this defect in her ; charming character was never entirely overcome. hg, j however, would not permit his friends to be isolated in a damp cavern, so it had remained unoccupied for a long I time. - .</p>
        <p>, For the rest of this amusing story see the April " Windsor Magazine," a very notable Easter Holiday Number, containing a large instalment of Anthony Hope's remarkable romance, " Sophy of Kravonia," besides complete stories by E. Phillips Oppenheim, Justus Miles Forman Frank Richardson, Hamilton Drummond, Frances Rivers and other clever writers. ; The selection from " Vanity Fair's" famous gallery this month includes some thirty celebrities of Bench ' and Bar reproduced in the original colours, and the pic- tures of Mr. J. C. Holtman form an interesting fine-art I subject. A great commercial question is treated under ' the title" Via Hudson Bay," The *hole number is superbly illustrated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CADDIES AND THE LABOUR MARKET.</head>
        <p>CADDIES AND THE LABOUR MARKET.</p>
        <p>Those who argue that golf links in a neighbourhood are responsible for the drawing away of boys from business and trade forget that there is one important industrial factor which has long been in operation to produce the young loafer of the village as well as of the big city. It is the decay of the apprenticeship system. Sub-division of labour by all kinds of machinery, the growth and power of Trade Unionism in imposing restrictions, have tended greatly to limit the opportunities of boys to enter the trades and learn the use of tools as their fathers did before them.— Country Life." THE STRAYED. Have ye ne'er a word for charity, Good neighbours who staidly fare ? I have lost my way unto Arcady, Who once was native there. I thought that the moon might point me true Or that young-eyed Spring would come With a laugh and glad' This way, my lad !" To lead me safely home. Surely I strayed but a little way On the path of sordid things. Oh, the road was plain till I turned again Where the sign of Wisdom swings. Never a bird may sipg to me now, "Follow me, follow and find." And never a rose the pathway shows Blowing adown the wind. Oh, land of my heart, my Arcady, Oh, land I am native to, Do you guess how far your children are Who wandered away from you? Homesick, bewildered and wistful all In alien lands we roam. We have lost our way unto Arcady, And none may lead us home. THEODOSIA GARRISON. In the" Smart Set." THE APRIL "GRAND.',</p>
        <p>A magazine that styles itself the "Grand," and prints on its cover that it is "the most original magazine in the world " and that" every page is interesting." has no easy task before it if it is to escape being dubbed an imposter apd a sham. Month after month, however, the " Grand Magazine" justifies both its name and its pretensions. The'April number, for instance, contains no less than seven stories by the very foremost writers of the day, and something like a score of articles that give the reader in amusing form precisely the informa- tion he wants to keep abreast of the complex movement of the modern world and make him feel that he is not out of it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NOBLEMAN'S SACRIFICE FOR LOVE..</head>
        <p>A NOBLEMAN'S SACRIFICE FOR LOVE..</p>
        <p>The April number of the "Wide World Magazine" is a most excellent one, packed from cover to cover , with contributions full of human interest and vitality. The issue opens with " Eventful Engagements,"Jeeing an account of some of the adventures which fall to the lot of music-hall artistes when visiting out-of-the-way parts of the world. Then we have" The Love of Count Erbach," setting forth the amusing sacrifice made by a young nobleman„ " for dear love's sake." The new series of marine romance. "On the High Seas," reaches its second instalment, which, if anything, is somewhat better than the first. The remarkable first-hand records here given prove conclusively that there is still excitement and adventure in plenty in the life of the seafarer.' People who are interested in the supernatural will be much impressed by " The Woman in Black," a doctor's weird story ; while in lighter vein, " Showing Him Round " narrate 4 the tribulations of a reporter who undertook to exhibit to a friend something of the seamy side of American city life. The remaining articles and stories are too numerous for individual mention, but their titles will give some indication of their interest. Thus we have " A Ten-Thousand Mile Race," "Among Insurgents and Brigands in Crete," "Across Mexico on Horseback." The Gasparini Mystery," etc.: etc. Altogether a splendid number of this uniquely interesting magazine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
