        <p>lIETTY, AND XLEANOML</p>
        <p>It is•ragrettable bow little space I have left myself .to discuss Serena, Iletty, Trix bind Eleanore. Serena inhabits a Parsonage " far from the madding crowd." She is not pretty,, only winsome—in hut, in outward semblance she reminds me very much of that delightful paiht- Mg of tOorge Romney's, banging in our National Gallery, whi,h is called, I think, " The Par- son's Daughter." 'Twonld seem that Ronnie) prophesied Serena. Iler life perhaps is onii lesser scale than that Of :Miranda with its con- quests and successes ; or of 311llicent's with its interest in large subjects, its purview of affairs and armaments, iiy omit rest even with these it might be called a " day of little things," a if of petty cares and pleasures. I prefer to call it a wild flower's existence. What has fortune. then, in store for this rattle primrose, this will violet " half hidden from the eye ?" To ls• plucked by homey lingers of some squireen or yeoman I Well,' so an honest heart beat in his breast., I must not mini-dein. And she I know will not. For she has no suspicion of her charm. Trix and lletty hunt in couples. 'They ar,- great visitors in country-houses, where yOu Inc trust them to keep things alive. Iff nniluestion- able good-nature, high pints still less (me... tionabie, they lire i,gi nl wliose society court. Because every o has his own peculiar taste, and mine is not t.-r practical jokes. Itut where Helirietta and ltestrix are. theme will 14. i apple-pie beds, there booby- traps, there !wilds dashed together as folks sir side by side urn, ottomans. Nor is that all, Wi.:ll, if it plesse. them— as to judge from their facile and in- cessant laughter it :mist do still I own I pre- fer the houses where these prang •ladies do visit.</p>
        <p>Eleanore is a dreamer---- fond of lien own emu. piny, which is iipt, to Intake la IWO) jet:1011. have known her, of tint, summer afternflotin, to spend hours beneath a wych' eltn-tree with no better company than a book of poems : a de. licions figure in a landscape, for she in hand. Kotile Mad dresses to perfection. But a• little tantalizing. Eleanore is htllCUOl'Ol' isa. lazy ylloald with it Sllggestioll of 16(1(1cm/ire. Dearly should I like to make her really a»gry. lit I'm afraid I don't' know .hcr :Nell enough. They SIC' she is of Creole extraction, whatever that may mean. Though I have made repeate-d at- tempts, I love never yet sin:eroded Igettlm: very much out of her. • I shall have aracther try some day. ,</p>
