        <p>SIR GEORGE DOUGLAS, linur., J.P., (Author hi " The i)irersons of a Cowley Gentleman,'' 4e.).</p>
        <p>Among gills I know the Queen of Beauty takes a natural precedence. This is intuitively recognised by mem and the women refrain from cballengiug, .it. Unit is the women of our Continental coterie, for I met Miranda at Dresden. Her Majesty has the gift. or shall I en y the art—it is prolstbly a combination of the two—of compelling homage. Wherever men and women are met together, her arrival, her approach, is not lees seeeible than the rise of the moon among night.c. `• ,aeaber beauties." 'the brightest etimrs are shorn of . something of their lustte. This arrive; will he nppropriately heralded and fittingly ilecompanied—there is a nutter of her characteristic diaperies, a little music of adorable eiclametions. before, as it were, she comes hi person on the scene. 'lids is the signal for immediate movement on the pert of all persons in the neighbianhood As though she could he in several places at (MU time, clue is made way for everywhere ; she lies her choice of chairs of all patterns. And she takes all this mupiestioningly, with charming condescension, as her due. Ilia has done much in helping her to become the queen she is.</p>
        <p>3111tANDA.</p>
        <p>I have spoken of her characteristic draperies . what is their character l• Essentially modish No ; essentildly feminine. She affects soft clinging materiels, eschews bard lines. One has never seen her, one cannot pieime her, wearing it tailor-made costume, or illrin!tt or boy's hat or cap. It would be a shock, no less, to do mo. Nor is her attire costly, for tin- good reason that her widowed moi her is by no means a .rich woman. Without richness, how- ever, she contrives to prod ice the effeet of rarity. She can do so mud, with a red rose, a pale blue ribbon, a white dress. It is Dot for a man to say how sine white dress differs frost another. lint how they,do differ ! Oh, what it risky thing it is for a woman to have a style of her 011.11 lit Ciret,t, ! queen Miranda, the Fit and Only, has dared this risk, and has emerged triumphant. Readers of any discrimation will have realised ere now thm Miranda is a blonde ; she is a blonde ri thevelroy rendrie. Eyes hazel ; figure womanly, almost more than girlish, though her age is twenty- two. What are t liranda's accomplishments: She has none. She does not play, or paint, or • lecite—these things are done by others for her delectation. 'that is, they draw ructures in her album, or COlllllWe SOngs ill her honour ; for be- yond this point her love for the arts does not. I think, extend. Only once have I seen her in hea ?wn person attempt it perfornamee ofKlly sort-- it Null a -performance on the castanets, which she is said to have studied at Seville. ~11, castanets are a pretty, "nit not st subtle or ex- acting musicals instrument. Still, hind a churl or a critic '(which is almost the same thing) been present, I fancy that he wcald have said that her success was one of charm rather than Of cit. lint 'Miranda disarms erdienmi. Perhaps. , on the whole, under this head 1 hardly do her Instice, for I am .sure that she could act—act, that is, it, the sense in which acting is under- stood by ninny popular actresses of the day. , As for the more active, or more ma,emioe complishments—lencmg, for instance, or horse- Inaliship—these are quite foreign to Miranda's</p>
