        <p>MILLICENT, Though Miranda may be doomed to single blessedness. I think that Millicent will nutrry well. 3Lillicent i 8 not a beauty like Miranda, she is merely handsome and striking : bill, well set up ; her ample brown • tresses, have strands or lights .of red in them ; eye,, of true grey ;it nr„,-ttion tint more brilliant Hum abundant. her style of dress is bolder, more durable --its lines have more of definition ilaul the chiffons and " beauty veils " affected by 31jranda. But then to Millicent life IllenllB a good deal more than mere queening it in a shifting Court of Beauty. Of the two young ladies she has the larger brain. Millicent impresses by distinction of bearing, you can see she is a gui of emnateler, her ' note," as writers say now-a-days—her note is easy power. Yet she remains pure feminine there is no trace about her of the man ni petti- coats—nothing even of that manifest self- sutlieinemess, which, useful though it may be, is certainty not what men as a rule admire most in a woman. Like Miranda, Millicent is not par- ticularly. artistic, but s 1 is interested in politics, and talks intelligently on the subjects discussed in the inure high-class reviews. At the sane time, to speak slangily, she has no intellectual 'side." I have even seen her defer her opinion to. 811 nridergraduate's--11undlity Kself could scarce do more. I remember I thought this very charming in her. But, indeed, L should not be surprised to learn that Millicent still clings to the outworn tradition of the superiority of my sex-. hid though i it were you would scarcely expect sue to condemn her kor this aberration.) She may have leant this rom her mother. And 31illieent is a very dutiful child. How 1 love to watch the gentle conflict at ides when she and the elder lady are at issue on some point ! From the very first one feels that con- viction on either side is beyond possibility—that here, in fact, ha a pair of affeetiouate irreeon- cilables. But Millicent's '` easy P