      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SPRING RASHES AND ERUPTIONS. --- Child's Painful Sores Yield to Zam-Buk.</head>
        <p>SPRING RASHES AND ERUPTIONS. --- Child's Painful Sores Yield to Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Children's Spring sicknesses are frequently accom- panied by skin troubles in the form of pimply eruptions or irritating rashes. Every mother should make thorough and frequent examinations of the little ones' skins, applying Zrun-Buk wherever there is the slightest sign of disorder. Such a course ensures skin-health. Mrs. Williamson, of 6, Upper Cyrus-street, off Gibbon- street, Manchester, said to a "Manchester Weekly Chronicle" reporter :—"A rash broke out upon my baby's head, and spread to the neck and shoulders. Then small sores came, which finally formed one big mass of eruptions. The sores seemed very painful and ran very badly. They irritated the child so much that he would keep trying to scratch them. "I got a box of Zarn-Buk. dressed the places thoroughly and regularly, and in a few days the sores began to dry. There appeared to be a discharge under- neath the crust, however, so I used to clean the sores carefully before each dressing, and at night I bandaged his head. I persevered with the daily treatment, and gradually the sores all dried up. I cut the dead and diseased skin away; new skin was grown by Zarn-Buk, and now the boy is quite clefiF ancd happy. His hair, which had fallen out, as the sores developed, has grown again. I only used three boxes of Zam-Buk, and it was never necessary to think of a doctor." If Zam-Buk is kept handy, and used for the many skin- ills so prevalent in the trying Springtime, it will avert much distress, disfigurement, and suffering, as well as doctors' bills. Adult skins get just as much good from Zam-Buk as do children's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>amßuk if RUB IT IN."</head>
        <p>amßuk if RUB IT IN."</p>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING MARRIAG E.—PRESENTATION 'TO MISS J. BENNETT.-011 Tuesday evening last a large audience assembled in the Wesleyan schoo lroo m,P ad gate, in order to witness a presentation to Miss J. Bennett on the occasion of her forthcoming marriage. Miss Bennett has been now for some years one of the organists of the church and has in addition rendered useful services in connection with the Guild and Sunday school, and her labours have been much appreciated. The members of the choir, congregation, Church and Guild and Sunday scholars therefore heartily joined together to recognise Miss Bennett's services and took the opportunity of her forthcoming marriage to give practical evidence of their appreciation. The presenta- tion took the form of a very handsome afternoon silver tea service. Mrs. Mellor, the wife of the resident minister, made the presentation on behalf of the donors and felicitous speeches were made by Councillor Brough on behalf of the choir, Dr. Bennett on behalf of the Church, congregation and Guild, Mr. F. Wright on behalf of the trustees, and Mr. Ward on behalf 3f the Sunday school. Miss Bennett in a very neat and happy speech appropriately responded. The Rev. A. C. Parker presided. We understand that Miss Bennett's future home will be in Lincolnshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FROM THE DOCK TO THE ALTAR,</head>
        <p>FROM THE DOCK TO THE ALTAR,</p>
        <p>A Halifax man was charged on Tuesday with fighting. It transpired that he was due to be married ten minutes later and the kindly Bench discharged both the com- batants, one of whom had to make a dash from the dock to the altar.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children, I am foißieg forward to receiving letters from you all, teiling me how you spent your Easter Remember that I have oftered a prize to the liuy and girl who sends me the best account of these vacations. lam sure you will all find plenty to tell me, especially those who have one in for our Flower Show Competition, V as I snail be most interested to hear if any of the seeds are coming up, I should like to know the sire and positian ot your garden, etc.. so please tell me everything and I will print the most in- teresting letters I receive. Write very neatly and distinctly ani in ink, anyone sending in con- tributions of any desciiption written in pencil will be disqualified. I am sending two prizes this week,,one to Millie Smith, and the other to Nellie Roberts, you will find their contributions printed below, and I think that both deserve a prize. I am sorry to keep so many of you waititig to see your stories in print, but as I have explained before, our space is limited, and you must all wait your turn. I wonder how many of you have received Easter Eggs? I should like to hear what they were, and who gave them to you, perhaps you will tell me about this in your holiday letter. I think I must conclude for this • week, I hope you have all had a very pleasant Easter, and that you all enjoyed your holidays. With all good wishes fiorm UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>--- : -.. : -</p>
        <p>and shook his head, dolly's very ill, of saiiklust, and he said, ttter keep her still.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle .Ned,</p>
        <p>HANNAH TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>- : ....- : -.</p>
        <p>I am. sending you this contribution so as _yon may not think that I have forgotten you. This. little story, which I hope you will like, is called</p>
        <p>HOMELESS.</p>
        <p>Drearily fell the rain in the city of Manchester one bitter winter evening. The streets were almost deserted, for no one cared to be abroad who could possibly help it in such weather. Market Street, so busy during the day with people bent on business or pleasure was very quiet now, ' and only now and then a passenger might be seen hurrying along. The church clock had just chimed ten, and the policemen on duty drew their wraps more closely round them as alittle protection from the cold and wet. Crouched in the shadow of a doorway, hoping, to escapd, the policeman's eye, or, worse still. Ins command to " move on,' was a little boy of scarcely eight. Poor boy! little pleasure had he known in his short life, still less of comfort, and hardly any love. And yet nestled close beside him was one—nay, perhaps the only creature in the world who loved him—a dog, a cur it would have been called by anyone but its loving little master. " Poor Nip," said the boy.gently, and stroked the shaggy back of his favourite. " Poor Nip, its very cold!" The child shivered as he spoke. and pulled closer the tattered rags he wore. He had a bundle of newspapers on his arm, and he counted them over and over again. " One, two, three, !Our, live, six, seven," said the child to him- self with a deep sigh. "What shall I do?" and the big tears dropped upon his hand. Nip whined uneasily, to express the sympathy he tett but could not speak, and rubbed his cold nose against his little friend's face. Bennie let the papers fall on the flags, tnd then put his arms roun;l the dog's neck.</p>
        <p>-Oh Nip," he cried, "nobody cares for me but</p>
        <p>s'Adiv sounded the childish tones on the cold night air, and he started in terror, fearing a police- man ought be near. But no, there he was, right tip the street ; and with a sigh of relief, Bennie , sib clasping Nip: drew farther into the doorway. Faster tell the rain, and colder blew the ‘vind, as the night wore on. Beanie hugged Nip closer to him and tried to get warm, hut it was of no use, by-and-bye he did not feel the colch for a strange sense of dr,,wsiness crept over him. Next morning, when daylight shone over the streets of Manchester, under the shadow of a dtKirway was found a little dead child, while a shaggy dog kept watch beside him. A heap of newsrapers, torn and wet with the storm, were lying near. They carried him away, and people wondered and pitied and told how he was louial, iwerybody said he had died of cold ond hunger.</p>
        <p>From NELLIE Romcwrs.</p>
