      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PROTEST AGAINST TFIE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting in connection with St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, was held at noon on Tuesday the Vicar (Rev. James Collin's) presiding. Amongst those present were the Rev. H. A. Lester' Mr. Henry Greensit (vicar's warden) and Mr. W. Richardson (people's warden). At the commencement of the meet- ing the Vicar expressed his gratitude to the congrega- tion for their Easter offering amounting to £24 Bs. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>FIWAS-C (AL.</head>
        <p>FIWAS-C (AL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson presented the balance sheet which showed that there was a balance due to the wardens of A:7 3s. 4d. The receipts amounted to £357 3s. 7d. as against £353 Os. 6d. last year and included £244 7s. 20. from general offertories. This latter surn,Mr. Richard- son explained, %vas .£2 less than last year and the special donations to the church showed a decrease of .£5 105.6 d. Extra cleaning had had to be undertaken at a cost of £4 ss. and there was a sum of 2s. for new surplices. On the other hand, a saving had been effected on several items.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. A. Down, seconded by Mr. A. J. ‘Villett, tho balance sheet was adopted.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS. The Vicar nominated Mr. H. Greenall to serve as his warden.</p>
        <p>• Mr. W. Richardson expressed a wish to retire hod the office of people's warden, but it was the unanimous wish of the vestry that be should continue to discharge the duties, and he was re-elected on the motion of Mr. Down, seconded by Mr. Sharrocks. The following were re-electeol sidesrnen :—Messrs. Arthur Down, J. Lee, J. H. Clarke, Thos. Sharrocks, S. W. Dallow, W. Mclntyre, A. J.' Willett, J. Parkes, Leonard Davies, John Jeffs, T. R. Westbrook, and J. McGregor. Messrs. J. McGregor and T. Sharrocks were re-elected lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>THANKS.</head>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thanks were accorded to all the church officers, the choir, organist (Mr. F. Rose), and deputy organist (Mr. A. Down) for their valuthle ser- vices. Special reference was made to the admirable voluntary work of the choir who, it was remarked, worthily upheld their reputation on Easter Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. A. Lester said that in view of the Educa- tion Bill which was now before the country they as an Easter vestry ought to speak their minds plainly and fairly on the subject. Before another vestry came round they would have to tight a battle, and they ought to be prepared for it. Ho moved the following resolu- tion :—" The Education Bill as proposed by Mr. Birrell is regarded as a violation of the two great principles of liberty of the subject and liberty of conscience and as such calls for the most strenuous opposition of all loyal Churchmen. No measure can be regarded as satisfactory which clop rives the Church of England parents of :heir undoubted right to have their children educated in the elementary I schools of the country in conformity was their con- scientious religious convictions by teachers approved by the Church." He suggested that the resolution should be forwarded to the Minister of Education and to the local member of Parliament. The time for half. measures and faintness had gone byand churchmen must be perfectly united and strenuous in their opposition to this unjust measure. The Archbishop had called them to arms in the matter and they %,velcomed the call. As loy tl sons of the Church they must be up and doing for the benefit of their own children and of children yet unborn. Mr. Dallow seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson in supportin,, said he did not suppose church people as a body would be passive resisters but they would certainly be active resisters and oppose the Bill by all legitimate means. The Rev. H. A. Lester said the Bill seemed to cut right through their trust deeds and the schools were gem,' to be seized. They were not to be tried by an ordinary Court but by some High Commission Court or Star Chamber. They must not wait until they got the halter round their necks and then cry out. Mr. Dallow : We were a bit too slow at the general election. I The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to the Vicar for presid- ing and also to the Rev. H. V. Pigot for his services prior to leaving the parish to become Rector of Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Vicar, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, presided over a moderate attendance at the Easter vestry meeting which was held at the Church on Tuesday evening.— The sta_tement of accounts showed that the total income during- the year had been £317 13s. Did. including general offertories amounting to 2124 3s. 9d. and special offertories £l5B 7s. 10d. The expenses totalled £325 19s. 33d. so that the year's working showed a debit balance of £8 ss. 4d.—rhe accounts were approved on the motion of Mr. Rose seconded by Mr. James Dunbabin.- -The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said that the wardens had looked after the Church during the time they bad held office and had put it in a condition of repair such as he had never before known. In this direction he thought Mr. Halton was to be especially congratulated. In the past he had taken upon himself the duty of initiating periodic changes in the wardens, and as Mr. Halton had held the office for some years he. had much pleasure in nominating Mr. William Gill et his warden for the ensuing year. Mr. Gill was a gentleman of ability who took a deep interest in the Church and he thought he would be of good service.—Mr. Welsby expressed a desire to be allowed to retire from the office of people's warden as he had held the position for several years, but in response to the unanimous wish of the vestry be consented to serve for another twelve months.—Mr. Halton was nominated as Mr. Gill's sidesman, Mr. Welsby appointing Mr. Harding.—The sidesmen were re-elected with the exception of Mr. Naylor, whose place was taken by Mr. Emmott and Mr. Wm. Gill, who was succeeded by Mr. Rowson. Other officers elected were: Lay delegates, Mr. R. W. Row- son and Mr. John Hesketh ; auditor, Mr. Naylor ; vestry clerk, Mr. G. F. F. White.—The Vicar proposed a vote of thanks to the officers for their past services. They could not have had gentle- men more attentive to their duties than the wardens who had served them during the past year. He desired to express his thanks to the choir whose services had , been much appreciated. The care they had taken in ' rendering the services had given the utmost satisfaction and he was glad to say that they were a harmonious body in more senses than one. (Hear, hear.) He also desired to thank the organists Mr. T. D. Amos,and Mr. Randall. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Rose seconded. He did not think he ever saw the Church look so well as it did at that time. Everything seemed to be in a most satisfactory condition. (Hear, hear.)—The motion was heartily approved.—A vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>GRAPENHALL. The vestry meeting waiheld on Tuesday morning in the vestry, the Rector (Rev. EL V. Pigot) presiding.— The retiring churchwardens (Messrs. Dakin and Lee Leyland) presented the accounts for the past year which showed an adverse balance of .£8 is. 3d.—The Rector appointed Mr. A. Lister. Kayo as his warden, and Mr. Lee Leyland was elected people's warden, and Messrs. George Warburton and Henry .Leyland sidesmen. On the proposition of Mr. Dakin, seconded by Mr. George Warburton, Messrs. Lee Leyland and Henry S. Leyland were elected lay delegates, ruri-decanal and diocesan conferences.</p>
        <p>ST, JOHN'S CHURCH, WALTON. The Rev. L. J. T. Darwall presided over the vestry meeting which was held on Monday. The church ex- penses' account showed a balance in hand. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and Mr. William Weir were reelected wardens. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Organist (Mr. F. Booth) and the members of the Church Choir.</p>
      </div>
