      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Dear Uncle Ned,</head>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,</p>
        <p>I am very pleased with my writing case. I never expected to get a prize at all. I have not had time to write before, because I have a lot of homework to do at night. I attend the Grammar School, and am getting on very well. The writ- ing case was delivered this morning while I was at school, and I was very surprised when I got home to find this present awaiting me. I did not notice it at first, but my' brothers and sisters accosted me with, "Oh 1 you are blind." After a time I found • out the reason for this shout. I shall now have enough in stationery, etc., to last me a considerable time: I shall try in other com- petitions when I have the chance. I must now close because I have some home lessons to do. Hoping to write again,</p>
        <p>I remain, yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>HENRY SIIERCLIFFE</head>
        <p>HENRY SIIERCLIFFE</p>
        <p>- : -.- : -</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle NO,</p>
        <p>I am now writing a short letter to you to thank you very much for the nice prize you sent me. I am sorry I did not .write before, I have read it twice and have. enjoyed it immensely. I, •am sending you a piece 9f poetry and a short story which I hope you will like. I will try to make those puzzles out. I see you have given some of your nieces and nephews some very nice prizes. I think this is all I have to say this time, so I will conclude. Witli,best wishes to you and all the members. 1 remain, your loving member,</p>
        <p>MILLIE SMITH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MEDDLESOME MARY.</head>
        <p>MEDDLESOME MARY.</p>
        <p>Mary was a dear little girl, but she would touch things she was told not to. One day she Was with her mother, who was making cakes in the kitchen. Mary was helping quite nicely, and made a little cake all by herself. .By and by her mother said, "1 must go upstairs to fetch my keys, don't touch anything while I am away. ' "No mother," said Mary. But no sooner had her Mother gonO than Mary's fingers began to long to touch something, and she felt she could not „.keep still. There was a large bag standing on a high table, and the little girl did long to know what was inside it. She got nearer ancinearer to the table, and at last up went her hand. The bag was rather heavy, so she tilted it on one side to see what was in it. But it was heavier than what she thought, and her hand was not strong enough to hold it back. It was a bag ct flour, and in a minute it came all over her. When her mother came back she found a little white girl crying, and, as her tears ran down her lace and mixed with the flour, she was a very odd sight indeed. "0.11 Mary, I told you not to touch V "I am sorry," sobbed Mary, and I think she really was, for she never did touch again when asked not to do it. •</p>
        <p>MILLIE SMITH.</p>
        <p>- : -...- : -.-</p>
      </div>
