      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>Two entirely opposite views of the present attitude of ladies towards cycling have been lately stated. In the Touring Club Gazette,' Miss Constance Everett Green, whose opinions are always worth reading, declares that to the spread of motors is due a decline in the feminine interest in cycling. Wealthy women use cars; many clean and prudent ones are shy of the dust and danger associated with the motors on the highways; while some nervous persons .give up for fear of accident, or out of consideration for the anxiety of -others. Miss Green points out that the sex and clothes of womenNare less suitable for exposure to dust or danger, and that it is difficult for them to cleanse their hair of the dm-t collected on the road. On the other hand,„3lr. Wheeler, writing in the 'People,' is .gratified to note the revival of interest in cycling shown by ladies, and forthwith tenders them much sound advice. Although certain main roads are unsuitable for use by lady cyclists on some days a the Week, there still remain abundant opportunities for much enjoyable riding nearly everywhere in the kingdom. Revival of Cycle Polo. Aithollgil attempts were made to foster cycle polo in England, some few years back, it never caught en, except as exhibited by certain pro- Jessional cyclists on music-hall stages, and on seaside piers. A specially prepared ground of smooth rolled sward, and special machines are necessary for the game to be properly enjoyed. In Dublin, a polo association flourished for a time and then died. Quite lately there has been a revival of enthusiasm, and, a suitable ground having been generously allotted for the purpose, the polo association is about to be reconstituted. Mr. B. J. Meeredy and about a score of others have formed the Vallombrosa Bicycle Polo Club, at Bray, and play :will take place on Wednesday and Saturday afternoons.</p>
