      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Care of Band Brakes.</head>
        <p>The Care of Band Brakes.</p>
        <p>Nothing could be more satisfactory than the effective action of a well-designed band brake, "whether applied, by band or foot. Occasionally, however, owing to the leather lining of the band becoming bardebed from previous exposure to wet, the power of the brake is seriously impaired. If this deterioration escapes the rider's notice until the brake be suddenly brought into • use, some risk of an accident is quite likely to result. All that is needed to restore the normal gripping efficiency of the band is an 'application of castor oil. After a winter's disuse, this small attention will probably suffice for the season's riding. Lubricating oil is Useless for the purpose above 'described.</p>
        <p>cyclist, sustained while being towed ,d's motor biercle, on the Portsmouth ts a word of T;,arnitto.. Only a skilful ....ent cyclist should accep't towing aid from a motorist. • The customary course is for the cyclist to ride on the off side of the tower, placing his left, hand on the •motorists right shoulder. This allows of instantaneous disconnection. In cases where one cyclist is towing another, the connecting cord should be fixed to the tower's saddle pillar: the other .end, after taking a half- turn round the steering pillar of the towed machine, being held in the hand. To avoid jerks, a section of the tow line should be elastic. When a descent is reached the line can be -cast off and gathered tip by the leader until again needed. Except in the ease of a badly baked ride., or against a stiff head wind! towing is little resorted to nowadays, the sliciter afforded in the wake of a strong rider being sufficient to ease the work of a hanger-on.</p>
