      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>DEALERS FOR CATALOGUE</head>
        <p>DEALERS FOR CATALOGUE</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>Bradford Beat Batley.</p>
        <p>Played at Huddersfield on Saturday before a crowd of 15,707 persons. A slight breeze blew down the field,and winning the toss, Batley had first use of it. The game was but ten minutes old when, from a scrummage on the Bradford line, tle ball was heeled out to Oakland. The Batley half back snapped it up, and darted past the opposing backs, scoring a really clever try. Wattle Davies was unable to land a goal, although the place was by no means difficult. The Bradford men, roused by this early disaster, now worked hard, and Sinton was twice prominent in smartly using touch. From a fiercely contested scrummage Brear slipped cleverly past his opponents and scored a fine try. Laidlaw missed the goal. Half-time :—Bradford, I try (3 points) ; Batley, 1 try (3 rloints).—Bradford now bad the use of the wind, and they took full advantage of it. The Batley backs were playing a remarkable "spotting" game, and though Marsden time after time tried to get his three-quarters in motion they could not get away. Finding his three-quarters were being too well watched to be able to do any damage, he made an effort to get through by himself. Seven minutes from the close he got the ball from a scrumrurt,,e, and dashing round he completely beat Oakland and Cookson and scored a magnificent try. The goal kick was a failure. Five minutes, later another scrummage was formed near the corner flag. Once more Marsden was put in possession of the ball, and again he heat his opposing halves in fine style, and went over at the corner. Final score : Bradford 1 goal 3 tries (II points) ; Daley, 1 try (3 points). Teams and officials : Bradford—Gunn, back ; Sinton, fieseltine, Connell and Dechan, three-quarter backs ' • Brear and Marsden, half backs ; Lnidlaw, Feather, Grednwood, Grayson, Smales, Bees, Francis and Sharratt, forwards. Batley—White, back ; W. P. Davies' Loosemore, W. T. Davies and Turton, three-quarter backs ; Oakland and Cookson, half backs ; Fryer, Kitson,Tebbutt, James Wade, Phillips, Hodgson, Edwards, tind Settle, forwards. Referke, Mr. .1. Fl. Smith, Widnes. Touch judges, Messrs. W. W. Taylor, Hunslet, and P. Asquith, Wakefield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Salford Qualify for the Final.</head>
        <p>Salford Qualify for the Final.</p>
        <p>Foethe first time, one of the Northern Union Cup semi-finals took place on the ' Wilderspool Ground at Warrington on Saturday. The local interest in the match, however, as manifested in the attendance, was not great, for despite the fact that the Keighley and Salford clubs, who were the semi-finalists, must have btought between them upwards of 5,000 supporters, the total attendance was only returned at 8,500, the receipts amounting to 246.—Keighley, won the toss and had the advantage of the wind during the first half. Keighley acted on the aggressive, and a penalty just within the half-way give Walker an opportunity of taking a good but unstfccessful shot at goal, the ball bitting the upright and bounding back into play. Two ruinots were forced in rapid succeEsien, and then a sudden rush initiated by Lornas transferred hostilities to the heighiey end. Keighley bad a narrow escape when MneWhirter knocked on after receiving a cross- kick from Lomas. From the succeeding scrummage, however, Salford ace ed, Pleston dodging over with a try which Brown failed to convert.Keighley tried hard to make up the leeway,but could not do so and at half-time the score thus stood—Salford 1 try (3 points), Keighley nil. The second half was started by Bradley, whose kick was not replied to, so that play settled for a time in the Salford half. Relief was obtained by Preston, and Salford established themselves within their opponents' half with the ultimate result that Preston ran over with a try, in which he beat both Stacey and Walker. The kick at goal was taken by Lomas, who failed. The hopes of Keighley supporters were revived when Myers, with a characteristic effort scored a try ten minutes' off time. Lomas failed with a drop at goal,and Preston only just missed a try in a final rush just on the call of time. Result :—Salford, two tries (six points) ; Keighley, one i.ry (three points). Teams : Salford—Coehranr, hack ; MacWhirter, Thomas, Lomas, and liampson, three-quarter backs ; John and Preston, half backs ; Rees, Rhapps, W Brown, Spencer, arwick, Thomas, Lewis and Buckler, forwards. Keighley Walker, back ; Stacey, Hardwick. Hardacre, and Jagger, three-quarter backs; Myers and Bateson, halfbacks' Bradley,Fearnley, Blades, Hopkinson, Pickles, Temptest, McNicholas, and Bairstow, forwards. Referee, Mr. Robinson, Manningharn. Touch jtrdges, Messrs. W. iJones, Runcorn, and C. Platt, Swinton. NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>St. Helens v. Warrington.</head>
        <p>St. Helens v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>The meeting of Tthese old rivals at St. Helens on Monday attracted a large crowd. Mercer kicked of altainst the snn. A pretty passing movement between</p>
        <p>The Cycling Slump in America.</p>
        <p>Cycling in the States has reached a deplorably low ebb. Just as the boom reached, its height in America before it was experienced in England, se the slump overtook them a few years earlier. The League of American Wheelmen, which once boasted 100,000 meltbers, Is no More, while only one cycling journal survives out of the big group of flourishing weeklies existent a decade ago. Despite -a vigorous Ride a Bicycle' campaign organized by makers and agents, little ground has been recovered, until it is now proposed to con- centrate attention on youths of 15. Notwith- standing the rapid advane.e4 of civilization in the cities, and the modern facilities of locomotion, the roads in the States remain in a very inferior con- dition outside the towns; and it is to this fact, that the decline of a once prosperous industry and popular pastime is ascribed. Bad roads have also done much to check the development of the motor trade across the Atlantic.</p>
        <p>uisance is in many of our suburban districts greater than ever. Dogs that have suffered bereavement from members of their families having been rolled flat. by motor cars, seem to delight to bark and bite at the inoffensive cyclist by way of revenge. Dog owners seem quite impervious to advice to keep their pets out of mischief, and the result is that many yelping canines meet motors and yelp no more. '</p>
