      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 21, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 21, 1906.</p>
        <p>Briers, Hißen, Toole, Harris, and Bates raised the enthu- siasm of tire home spectators, but Bates was obstructed by Fish. Little advantage was gained from the free kick. The St. Helens forwards were playing in sur- prisingly good form, and put on pressure. borne ex- citement was caused by Fish getting away, but he was promptly surrounded. Harris made a clever mark, and 'Toole and Prescott were collared in front of the post. From the scrimmage Toole had a good chance of dropping a goal, but was a bit slow, and his kick was charged. Hockenball finally relieved the pressure to half way, and the home men dribbled up the field, lint Ashcroft opened out the play, and the ball was seneon to Fish, who kicked down and followed on,knocking the leather out of Foulk es's band and scoring behind the post. He also kicked a goal. Half-time : Warrington 5 points, St. Helens nil. Warrington in the second half had to play against the wind, but they began to make the game more of a forward description, and for sonic time were engaged at the St.Helens end,and Hockenhall ran close up to the goal, where Foulkes was lucky to collar him. A scrimmage followed, and Ashton received a wide pass and walked over while the home team were appealing for pass-forward. No goal was kicked. Play was then carried on for some time at the Warrington end, but by superior speed and dash Warrington drove the homesters back, and Berry made a big attempt to get over. Result :—Warrington 8 points, St. Helens nil.</p>
        <p>up quite as effectually but without hurting anybody. , Ile moved that the first clause should be altered to read : "That in the opinion of this committee the existing arrangements for the medical treatment of the sick indoor poor are not satisfactory," Mr. FORSHIW accepted the amendment. Mr. BENT moved the deletion of the first clause. He', did not think such a resolution should be brought for- ward until they knew that the Local Government Board would allow such measures as were recommended.</p>
        <p>Only the proposer and seconder voted for the amend- ment and the committee's resolution' with the amendment of the first clause suggested by Mr. Houghton, was approved. Mr. HOUGHTON said the question of supplying infor- mation to the Press had troubled them at Padgate from time to time and it ought rellly to be put a stop to. He hoped the culprit would acknowledge it in this , instance sooner or later, and he stiggested the sending out of postcards to every member asking for a direct reply as to whether he supplied the information. The CLERK pointed out that only one person could have supplied the information and to put the question to all the members would be an insult. Mr. Awbery, as the only official present at the meeting of the House Committee, said he was in no way respon- sible for the publication of the minutes which he always considered should be confidential before they were sub- mitted to the Board.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said the practical effect of the Board's letter was that they would allow them. The Board said-, they could allow two years salary and emoluments and that they would favourably consider the decision if the Board came to that conclusion. Mr. BENT said the continual bringing forward of the matter tended to make the Board look ridiculous in the eyes of the public. Mr. Am-set:tom seconded and said that when thei matter went before the Local Government Board the Guardians would be the laughing stock of the whole country and they would deserve it. He had been a member of the House Committee for eight years and he had never 'mown a case to be brought before that cora- mittee in which Dr. Braid° had neglected attending to , his duties. If the doctor had neglected attending to his ; duties it was the place of the Guardians to bring cases before the committee. To pass such a resolution,when they had no cases before them, was a shameful affair altogether. It was he contended, due to two or three individuals. He should do his utmost to find out who I -- supplied the information to the Press as he looked upon' SHE DISCARDED HER CRUTCHES! ! it as an attempt to ruin the doctor in his practice. 59, Mount-street, Northwood, Mr. HICKMAN said he should support the recomd Hanley, Staffs, mendation of the committee from reasons which had 1 Jan. 6th, 190:5. Dear Sir,—My wife suffered from Rheumatism for a been advanced and discussed in committee. He repu- diated as a member of the House Committee that there long time, and could get no relief. She was a patient was any attempt on the part of that committee or of at the North Staffordshire Inlirmary_, heard about your but got-tvorse,and the board to injure Dr. Braid° in the slightest degree, could not walk without crutches. I He would not be a party to the resolution if he thought Pills from a friend, and bought a small battle. AFTER there was any such intention—in fact he would he the THE SECOND Doas SHE DISCARDED HER