ection with the treatment of the sick indoor poor .for several years past. Any member who had been a member of the Board for six years and bad the effrontery to get up in public and make a statement like that with the knowledge of what had happened there and what was on the records of that Board and of the committee placed himself in a category which the decencies of public discussion pre- vented one from adequately describing. He would con- tent himself by saying that the statement was simply not true and any member who had paid any real atten- tion to the administration of the institution during the past seven years knew perfectly well that tThe state- ment was not true. Mr. Asuottorr : Give us names. Mr. JOLLEY : I can give names and dates, but I don't propose to do so. They stand on public record in Warrington and in London. If some members of this Board who seem so very anxious to have a mani- festation of courage want to display their own courage a simpler plan would be instead of moving the deletion of this revised section number one to have suggested that the word " uot " should be struck out. That would have been a definite expression of opinion that the arrangements are satisfactory. Either they are or they are not. Continuing, he said that for eight months the House Committee bad been trying over and over again to improve matters: When they brought forward a scheme of a necessarily tentative character they were reviled for not producing a com- plete- scheme and the matter had been referred back several times. The Board as a whole had com- mitted itself almost unanimously to the opinion that things were unsatisfactory. What was happening now was simply an honest attempt to find n solution of the difficulty in which thesadmitiletration of the institution was placed. He submitted it was very desirable that personalities should be eliminated from the discussion of a matter of urgent public importance. What they ought to think about were not personal feelings but the interests of the sick poor whom they were sent there to look after. He thought Mr. Hickman had pointed out sufficiently clearly that the suggestions that mem- bers were actuated by personal feelings were more than repudiated by the recommendation of the committee as to the granting of compensation. It was not a question of mere fairness. It was as generous treatment as could be submitted by any public body dealing with on official. The first clause of the resolution was the pivot on which the whole thing turned. None of the members of the committee bad any ill will whatever. Their one aim had been to im- prove the treatment of the sick poor and the adminis- tration of the institution as a 'whole.</p>
