      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A" v. ST. HBLENS " A."</p>
        <p>Great interest centred in this match at Warrington on Monday as Warrington had either to make a draw or win the match to make sure of winning the" A" team championship. There was a large attendance. St. Helens were out forty-five minutes late, and when play commenced the opening stages were evenly contested. Knight, the home full back, =dee bad mistake, almost letting the Saints in. After passing by the backs, Kenyon was tackled on the line, and a minute later Lambert was also held up near the post: Jordan at length broke through at the corner' Knight failing at the goal. The home team continued the attack, and Lambert dodged over after smart passing with Kenyon. Half-time : Warrington" A"G points, St. Helens "A" nil. In the second half Warrington [held the upper hand and increased their lead by 16 points, St. Helens being unable to score.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>CRITCHLEY (WIGAN, CUP. _- REPLAYED FINAL. WALKER'S BREWERY VI HIGLiFIELD.</head>
        <p>THE CRITCHLEY (WIGAN, CUP. _- REPLAYED FINAL. WALKER'S BREWERY VI HIGLiFIELD.</p>
        <p>At the second time Of asking Walker's Brewery team on Saturday accounted for Wigan Highfield, and the large crowd present at the Central Park enclosure, Wigan, on Saturday, had the satisfaction of witnessing an dacellent game. The teams had met the previous week when Highfield were considered very lucky in effecting a draw in the last few minutes. No mistake was made by Walker's men on Saturday, and entering with spirit into the game they won by two tries to nil. On returning to Warrington with the cup the victorious team paraded the town and met with an enthusiastic reception.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Election of Chairman and Vice – Chairman.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Election of Chairman and Vice - Chairman.</p>
        <p>DR. BRAIDE'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday. At the commencement of the proceedings the clerk (Mr. J. C. Sutton) presided and there were also pre- sent :—Mrs.' Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, W. Hickman, J. Billinge, E. L. Bent, T. H. Ashcroft, It. Bolton, S. Davies, W. Houghton, J. Backhouse, J. Cook, G. Mather, R. Flannagan, M. W. Seton, A. Brooks, T. Ritchie, J• C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Cottage Homes). ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN. Mr. DAVIES said that when he was comiry, to the meeting he was told to his surprise that there was going to be a full dress debate on the question of the chairmanship and that they must look out for storm. (Laughter.) He was, however, very glad now to feel that they were going to get over the business very smoothly. S,nne thought there should be a change in the chairmanship but he did not, Mr. Billinge had served them so well during the past year that he had pleasure in proposing his re-election. Mr. HOUGHTON seconded, and referred to the extremely satisfactory manner in which Mr Billinge bad discharged the duties. Mr. E. L. BENT supported and the motion was unani- mously passed. Mr. BILLINGE returned thanks, and said he respect- fully solicited a continuance of the valuable help of his fellow-members in the conduct of the business. He hoped the year upon which they had entered would be a very happy and prosperous one to each of them.</p>
        <p>. . - THE VICE-CHAIRMAN. On the motion of Mr. FottstrAw, seconded by Mr. Asitcnorr, Mr. Hickman was unanimously re-elected vice-chairman. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>..... . In responding, Mr. HicKmas said that if the Board would pardon his past infirmities be would promise to he" a good lad " in the future. If occasion did arise for him to occupy the chair in Mr. Billinge's absence he would try to be as graceful, good and impartial as Mr. 13illinge was.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The nest business was thesppointment of committees and with one or two exceptions the old committees were re-elected. _</p>
        <p>It was decided that the Board should he represented at the Central Poor Law Conference by the Uhairruan, vice.clthirman and clerk, and at the Conferena of the Association of Poor Law Unions by Mr. A. J. Jolley_arld the clerk. DR. BIZAIDE'S POSITION. At a meeting of tbe House Committee the following :01yrnmoniteteedistshenetieeuxtis—ti(nig) medical treatment of the sick indoor poor is not Thatsatis- Tes ol tni rh se NoVpeirneiop na s s oe fd wt ti sh factory—(2) That therefore the Local Government Board be asked to sanction fresh arrangements includ- ing the appointment of a Resident Medical Officer to be responsible for the Medical and Surgical treatment of the indoor poor, of the indoor stal,and for the training of the nurses. (3) That the Guardians consent to the paYment to Dr. Braide, as compensation for loss of office, the maximum amount allowed by law. Mr.FonsHaw moved the confirmation of the minutes. With regard to the resolution referring to the Medical Officer, most of the members of the House Committee had felt for a long time that matters with regard to the medical department were riot in a very satisfactory condition. In the early stages of this business, before the negotiations' with Dr. Braide commenced, Dr. Braide attended a meeting of the House Committee and a question was put to him from the chair as to whether he was satisfied with the condition of things that obtained in the Infirmary as to the medical department. His answer was that he was not satisfied. He (Mr. For- shaw) did not suppose that any Guardian would be bold enough to accuse Dr. Braide of neglect. He was sure the doctor had attended regularly,as the entries in the visiting book showed. He had come every time he had been sent for and he (Mr. Forshaw) was sure the doctor would repudiate most indignantly any assertion that he was guilty of any neglect of duty. Ho d.d not think any Guarilian would be bold enough to make that asser- tion. The fact, however, remained that the condition of things so far as the medical department in the Infirmary was concerned was not satisfactory, both in the opinion of the Committee and of the doctor himself.] The House Committee and the doctor, with the sane- tion of the Board, had been seeking a basis of mutual agreement as to the termination of the doctor's appointment, and they thought they had arrived at that basis on one occasion bnt the Local Government Board would not consent. 1 According to the letter of January lst tho Board would not sanction any conditions except the payment of two years salary and emoluments. He must refer to 1 the fact that the gist of the minutes of a recent House Committee meeting when the matter was discussed appeared in a local newspaper although no reporters were present. Either a member of the committee or an official must have given the information and he did not like such procedure. He did not think the person who supplied the information had done so through any ill- feeling and it was the duty of th'st person to stand up ' and say why he took such a course. Mr. JOLLEY seconded. Mr. Hot/OIITO'N expressed indignation at what he considered the babyish action of the member who sup- plied the information to the Press and which led to the newspaper that was victimised having to apologise. He would like to call attention to the unhappy drafting °Mho minute. He thought it might have been drawn</p>
