      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication most in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>" No Jurisdiction in this Realm" Sir,—The Establishment is the keeper in ordinary of those national types and blocks from which Popery is ever to be printed off; of the traditiona!, view of every Catholic doctrine, the traditional account of every ecclesiastical event, the traditional lives of popes and bishops, abbots and monks, saints and con- fesSors, the traditional fictions, sophisms, calumnies, mockeries, sarcasms and invectives with which Catholics are to be assailed. . . . It 'agrees to differ with its children on a thousand points ; one is sacred—that his Majesty the King is the Father and Master of all Churches ; on one dogma it may surely rest without any mistake, that "the Bishop of Rome hath no jurisdiction in this realm " ! Here is sunhine amid the darkness, sense amid the confusion, an intelligible strain amid a babel of rounds; whatever befalls, here is sore footing, It is" No peace with Rome," Down with the Pope," and" The Church is in danger." Never has the Establishment failed in the use of these important and effective watchwords ; many are its shortcomings, but it is withont reproach in the execu- tion of its special charge. Heresy and scepticism, and infidelity and fanaticism may challenge it in vain ; but fling upon the gale the faintest whisper of Catholicism, and it recognises by instinct the presence of its con- natural foe. " Ronta patiens quia neterna." Yours Ate., J. W. CAVANAGH.</p>
        <p>Golden Square, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. F. Matthews Replies to Questions. Sir,—l will answer Mr. Whitley's questions as far as possible, with your further kind permission. 1. Schools financed by the State, whether under the monopoly of Roman Catholics or Anglicans are "State Schools." This is certainly proved by the new Act placing them under complete public control. Parlia- ment cannot so deal with schools that are private property absolutely. 2. It is the ditty of the trustee to see that the Trust illbarried out of course'. It wonld not be his duty to "lock the doors," even on Mr. Woitlev's hypothesis. (1.) Because" to lock the doors" is not the only alternative. (2.) The school building was not erected solely fer religious instruction that is only part of the trust. (3). Further such grants to which Mr. Whitley refers,—and T understand them in the same sense as he does—do involve the observance of definite conditions. But" it is not the end of the matter so fat as Govern- ment control is concerned." For Voluntary Schools having thus become an increasing charge upon public funds, their full administration by the public authority has become imperative and inevitable. In answer to Mr. Whitley's last point, I would refer him to the letter of the great Wesleyan Educationist Rev. William Wakinshaw in his reply to the Archbishop of Canterbury's stricture on the Education Act, pub- lished, I believe in the "Manchester Guardian "the day following its copy of his Grace's letter to Mr. Talbot Baines.—l BILI very truly yours, JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEVVS. Latchford Baptist Church, April 18th, 190 G.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Association Football.</head>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>- Sir,—My letter to yoll of a fortnight ago asking "Why is there not a Warrington Association Football Club" has brought a lengthy reply from Mr. T. P. Bradsha..v,the Secretary of the Warrington and District Football Association. 'Unfortunately he has twisted my letter with its simple question into a denunciation of his association. I had no intention of doing so. It would have been much better if the question had been dealt with by gentlemen who are not paid to represent a particular association. as then there would probably have been less anxiety shown towards going out of one's way, to defend some body politic from a bogey created for the specific purpose of writing an autobiography. Seeing, however, that Mr. Bradshaw has entered the arena prepared 'to defend the Warrington and District Football Association against all corners. I should like with your permission to reply to several of his observa- tions and to ask him one or two pertinent questions. His letter is pessimistic throughout. He commences with a regret that I have not signed my name to my letter. I cats assure him that my modesty prevented me. I was not seeking self-advertisement, but was merely desirous of attempting in my own humble way, to centralise local public opinion so as if possible, to start without further delay a Warrington Association Football Club. A suitable name does not matter much, so long as the players are all right. Even the colours and field and things, can be arranged afterwards. At least I think so. With reference to what he calls my chief grievance, viz., the invitation to dine with the Executive Committee. I would like to inform him that the players are not anxious to be "patronized," but are quite able and willing to pay their fair share of the expenses. As to his remark that a levy has been made on the execntiye committee, to pay for those whom he calls outsiders, perhaps he will be good enough, to inform vonr readers how many outsiders were fed at the officials' expense. There should he no necessity for such a levy. He might also tell them whether two members of the executive committee have been appointed auditors, as according to rule 22, all accounts above £2. shall he brought before the execritive,and if approved be paid. He surely does not think that it is quite in order for the accounts to be audited by two members of the committee which has the spending of the money. Everybody recognises the usefulness of such en association, but seeing that it does not exist for the purpose of forming clubs,l am afraid that outsiders will have to take the matter up, If it is going to be an accomplished fact. I had honed that Mr. Bradshaw, who doubtless has had a good experience of the game. might have sug- gested some means whereby a meeting could have been arranged and assistance given. Because once upon a time a club was formed and failed that is no reason surely why further attempts should not now be made. I contend that a representative rommitte void of cliques can assure a successful club being formed. I shall be pleased to help.—Yourq truly,</p>
        <p>FULL BACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PROSECUTION OF A TOWN</head>
        <p>PROSECUTION OF A TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR.</p>
        <p>Date Fixed.</p>
        <p>We understand that May 3rd has been fixed for the preliminary hearing at the Borough Police Court of the charge against a Warrington Town Councillor tinder the Corrupt Practices Acts. Mr. Rigby Swift, Livernool, has been instructed for the prosecution by the Deputy-Town Clerk acting for the Corporation. It is reported that Mr. F. R. Roberts, who is at pre- sent on holiday in Gre&amp;T,will have charge of the C 330 for the defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Madden on Roman Catholic Policy.</p>
        <p>In the course of a letter which appears in to-day's (Friday's) "Manchester Guardian," Mr. Wm. Madden says: .\ Sir,—ln the beginning of February, just on the eve Of the opening of Parliament, I addressed you two letters on the srbject of the then Catholic attitude towards the new Education Bill, and referred, in strong terms, I admit, to what I considered was a want of the necessary action on the part of the Catholic bishops in giving their people a decided lead. Asa consequence of nay temerity in doing so, although I had been asked to support one of the resolutions of the great Catholic parents' meeting in the Free Trade Hall on the sth March last, I was objected to as a speaker by my ecclesiastical superiors, and therefore took no part in the meeting. lam not, however, to be intimidated by such methods. I would suggest that without one moment's delay ' their Lordships should summon a grand Catholic con- -I', ference of ecclesiastics and laymen, to be held at some central point. Their Lordships might instruct their ' priests in the lfi dioceses to call a meeting in every parish throughout England, to elect so many delegates from each diocese to a great meeting,say,at Birmingham ' at which would also he present the Bishops themselves the Trish Catholic members of Parliament, the Catholic ' members of the House of Peers, the heads of Catholic •, colleges, and other Catholic priests and laymen interested in and conversant with the education question. Such a Catholic conference, speaking with the voice • of the Catholics of England, could finally settle the 'irreducible minimum" of the Catholic claims and found an organisation to determine the line of Catholic [t action, and to lead the people in the subsequent fight, , should such become necessary. From such a conference a deputation could be sent to the Prime Minister with the Catholic ultimatum. We should then know where 1, St'e were. I am sorry to have to 'make the comment that had the . Catholic bishops of England shown a more cordial and i ' sympathetic spirit towards the Irish Parliamentary ' party,co-operation with the latter would be more easy now, and in the past would have produced more fruit , for the Church than their system of leaning upon a , broken reed like the Duke of Norfolk, who fails them - in a time of need.—Yours, &amp;e., Assize Courts, 31a.nchester, April 19th.</p>
        <p>MADDEN.</p>
        <p>lAay</p>
        <p>:f ,1-1</p>
      </div>
