      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ON " Ovalist "</p>
        <p>Football is getting flat and uninteresting in this district, and the close of the season will not be unwelcome when it arrives. There are only a few belated League fixtures left, and on the Warrington card there is only one, that with Hunslet at Wilders- pool on the last Saturday of the season. Practically, the season at Warrington closed last week-end with the Halifax match on Friday and the semi-final on the Saturday. Neither was a match to enthuse about and those people who went away for the week-end missed nothing they need regret.</p>
        <p>It seems to have become the customary thing for Warrington and Halifax to draw when they meet in League football, and both the matches this season have resulted in an equal division of points. On September 20th at Halifax Warrington were ten points in al rears soon after half•time, but they came up with a rattle in the closing stages and equalized matters. The Good Friday match here resulted in each side scoring a goal, while last season there was no score at all.</p>
        <p>- _ Halifax make a more resolute show at Warrington than any team which visits the Wilderspool ground and rarely indeed fail to give the home side a close struggle for supremacy. Unfortunately their efforts as a rule are in the direction of checking tendencies to score and replying on defensive tactics. This does not conduce to an'interesting display, and the game on Friday last was not worth going far to see. Principal interest centred around the full-back duel between Little and Berry, and it was very gratifying to find the Warrington man in such brilliant form that he more than held his own against his famous adver- sary. Little, however, kicked a goal from beyond the half way- line which must rank as one of the best individual feats ever performed on the Warrington ground.</p>
        <p>- The semi-final for the Northern Union Cup passed off very successfully at Warrington, and acc)rding to expectations Salford beat Keighley, but they were no better than the score, 6-3, indicates. On the form shown one cannot possibly fancy them for the Cup against such a team as Bradford. They will make their fourth appearance in the final and probably lose again. This seems hard luck on the Reds, and cer- tainly it is, but they have never been the team to com- mand confidence at the last hurdle. This year they are probably one of the poorest sides which ever qualified for the 'final round. Apart from Lomas, Preston, and John, the team is very ordinary, and on Saturday last, the Keighley men were rather the better individually. Their combined work was so slowly executed, however, that they missed the oppor- tunities which might have reversed the result. As a spectacle the match was poor, cup-tie methods pre- vailing throughout to the exclusion of clever, open football. I think Warrington could have given either side 5 points and won.</p>
        <p>-- Notwithstanding the glorious weather the gate fell below expectations, though it was not a poor one by any means. 0yer78,200 people paid for admission and the receipts were £248. Warrington's 10 per cent share will hardly pay expenses, but the Club did the right thing in securing a semi-final for the ground.</p>
        <p>--- Easter Monday saw the 'Warrington men at St. Helens, where they accomplished a meritorious per- formance in winning by 8 points tn nil. The Warring. ton team had C. Ashton at full back, Hockenhall and Hewitt at half-back, with Fish, Berry, Dickenson and Taylor threequarters. Later on Berry went full back, Ashton wing, and Taylor centre. The two tries came from Fish and Ashton on the wings, and Warrington were always the superior side.</p>
        <p>-- The League table up to date is as follows : For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage Leigh Hunslet 29 23 604663 68 330 17 31 127 7911 Leeds .......33 21 7 25048 91 369 25 22 116 75/5 i Oldham Keighley 27 18 8 13739 58 252 25 38 164 68651.1 'Wigan . Hull 36 22 11 34739 56 246 46 42 218 6527 Halifax.-- 36 19 9 84643 55 251 37 45 209 6388 Illnncorn I Hnd'seld 2B 16 10 25430 52 216 27 36 162 6011 Bradford 32 18 12 23846 81 335 32 40 184 6012 Brn'ton R. 31 18 12 13743 87 347 35 45 205 59.67 Swinton 3l 17 11 33728 49 203 23 35 151 5967 St. Helens 28 15 12 13134 55 233 32 43 193 5535 Warrington 37 18 16 33942 57 255 29 42 182 52.70 , Salford 3O 13 14 32934 57 239 42 45 219 48131 T 3213 15 43037 38 188 38 62 262 4617 Hull 35 15 19 13145 65 285 38 48 220 4428 Pontefract 26.11 14 12334 46 206 30 41 183 4423 Bramley 25 914 22024 26 136 30 61 243 40.00 York Batley 32 918523 37 28 158 40 41 212 35'901 Dewsbury 34 11 21 22422 32 140 40 56 248 3529 ! Barrow 3O 918321 16 34 134 44 76 316 35•00 Widnes 24 715216 11 26 100 25 51 203 3313 I Normanton 24 418 210 712 50 41 65 280 20131 Castleford 20 315 2 812 7 45 47 77 325 20001 Rochdale II 31 322 61212 27 105 31 76 300 19151 Brighouseß 25 320 2 815 18 81 42 79 321 1600 I Morecambe 25 2 19 4 8 12 25 99.35 68 274 1600Millom 1 19 215 2 6IA 13 67 45 77 32:3 15•781 •The points are two for awin and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible. -- Warrington " A " team secured ,the Combination Championship for the second.. year in succession on Monday when they beat St. Helens " A " at Wilders- Pool by 22 points to nil. Their only rivals for the leadership were Wigan " A," and the com- parative records of the two teams now that the 'fixtures have been completed are as follow : Played. Won. Lost. Drawn. Pts. Warrington ... 14 ... 11 ... 2 ... 1 ... 23 Wigan 14 ... 10 ... ... 1 ...</p>
        <p>The Works Competition has reached the semi-final stage. and the four remaining clubs will play the: semi-finals at Warrington en Saturday afternoon, viz.: ernsfield's v. Greening's, 3.30 ; Loco's v. Fletcher's, 4.30. The final will take place on Friday evening next week at C).30 when the medals both for the Works Competion and the Junior League will be presented.</p>
        <p>The South-west Lancashire League table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>Points.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Wigan Leigh Warrington lO . 4 0 . 84— 31 ... 12 Runcorn Widnes lO 3 • 7 0 34 93 ... St. Helens</p>
        <p>Many of the weaker clubs have been getting cheap points out of the Cup contestants in League games, and the practice of playing reserve teams still con- tinues. On Thursday Salford sent a weak team to Widnes with the result that the " chemicals " won easily by eleven points. This sort of thing is dead against the interest of the game, and a sterner remedy than and fines will have to he devised.</p>
        <p>-- Walker's Brewery team successfilly accounted for Wiqan Highfield in the re-play at Wigan on Saturday. and time; secured the Wigan Cup. The brewery me.n must be accounted one of the smartest. Junior teams in th,4 district on their performances_ this season.</p>
        <p>Cycling. The weather conditions during the Easter holiday. were the most, favourable experienced for some years Although there WaS n. slight chill in the atmosphere, the bight sunshine made it exceedingly , pleasant' to he awheel and cyclists bad a thoroughly enioyable,week- end. About eight members of the Warrington Cycling Club had a tour in North Wales. Saturday even- ing was spent at Ellesmere in Salop and on Sunday the party proceeded through Oswestry, Gobowen and birk, to that delightful resort. Llan- gollen. On Monday Carrog was visited, and the return Journey was made via Ruston, Whitchurch, and Sand- bach to Warrington, the tour haying been a complete stICCOBS. Another section of the club spent the week-end at Farndon-on-Dee, whilst a third party made their headquarters at Port St. Mary, Isle of Man.</p>
        <p>To-day's ran is to Helsby.</p>
        <p>Cricket. To-day (Saturday) Warrington open their season by a friendly match with Ashton-under-Lyne, on the Arpley field. During the past few weeks the players have been busy at the nets and good form has been shown. The opening match should result in a victory for the home team.</p>
