        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Continued. from. Page G.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. THE EDUCATION BILL. The annual vestry meeting in connection with War- rington Parish Church was held in the National Schools, Church-street, on Tuesday evening the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presiding over a large attendance which included Councillor J. G. Craik (Rector's warden), Mr. S. Taylor (people's warden), and Mr. E. A. White (vestry clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>This being the season of the year for the general potting of plants, a few remarks with regard to the work will no doubt be of interest to persons who have had no experience in the matter. For such plants as the geranium, fuschia, and other window ',plants, the soil should consist of loam 3 parts, leaf soil or decayed manure 2 parts,with a little sand to keep the whole porous. These should be thoroughly mixed together. For palms or aspidistras, a stronger soil is required, say 3 parts to one of decayed, leaves, as they do not require to be repotted so often as the plants mentioned above. Now with regard to size of pots required, that is a matter more for the " operator" to decide, but in many cases if ,the plants appear to be in a healthy condition, a size larger or even two sizes will be required. The pots should be perfectly clean inside, as well as outside, because if the pot be dirty it is impossible to take the plant out without damaging the roots, when required to do so on some future occasion. Every plant pot should be provided with a hole at the bottom to take away waste water. When potting, place over the hole, hollow side downwards, a piece of broken pot or oyster shell, with several more small pieces around, to ensure good drainage, next place a little rough soil, and then put the plant in position, in centre of the pot, place thelfresh earth round,and mate moderately firm by pressing the soil down with the thumb, leaving about half an inch of space from the top of the pot for water. When the potting is completed the plant should be watered carefully, so as not to wash the loose soil out of the pot. Always use rainwater if possible. When potting palms, always make the soil more firm,as having strong roots, they often lift themselves out of their pots unless made very firm. Should at any time the plants become very dry, place the pot overhead in a bucket of water and let it remain for twenty minutes,so as to saturate every particle of soil. The same remark applies to hanging plants or baskets. There is one thing often done which should be avoided, that is the placing of saucers under flower pots, and allowing water to stand in them for several days. This is a great mis- take. Where these thing are necessary they should have the water taken out, and the saucers washed and replaced every day. I trust these remarks will assist all persons who are trying to grow plants, to beautify their homes. R.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB V. CAtROLIC Y.M.S.</p>
        <p>The return match bet3veen teams representing the Liberal Club and the Catholic Young Men's Society was played in the rooms of the latter, Elorsemarket- street, on Thursday evening. The Liberals had a majority of 75 points from the games a fortnight ago On their own tables, but the Catholics were hopeful of reversing the position. Instead they were heavily defeated at home, for the Liberal Club men ron nine games out of twelve, and increased their majority from 75 to 251 points. Both contests were carried out in an excellent spirit, and the Catholics proved them- selves "good losers." The detailed scores for Thursday night are as follows: I LIBERAL CLITIL CATHOLIC V.M.S. lAlderman Tinnion Dr. B. Smith G. Marshall W. Green ... E. V. Walker lOO J. Durkin G. Millington J. Davies (capt.) W. Patrick lOO P. Corless E. Wilson W. Woods H. Somerville J. Chappell 1115</p>
        <p>939 Liberal majority 176, majority on home and away matches 251.</p>
      </div>
