        <p>forefathers for the benefit of posterity. (Applause.) The resolution was cordially approved. The Rector briefly reviewed the financial position, pointing out that £4OO was still to be raised for the St. George's Mission extension. He expressed his indebtedness to Mr. F. M. Darby for his valuable help in undertaking the Men's Bible Class. With regard to the Chancel, he hoped to bring something definite before the next Church Council meeting. ST. PETER'S CHURCH. The Rev. B. Percival presided over a small attend- ance at the St. Peter's Church vestry meeting held on Wednesday evening. The balance sheet showed that the year commenced with a balance in hand of Os. 3d. , The total income had been £l7B 15s. o,cl. and the year ended with a credit balance of £5 Os. 7d.—The Vicar thought they had every reason to be encouraged by the result of the year's work. He expressed his thanks to the officers-of the church and re-appointed Alderman John Roberts to act as his warden during the next twelve months.—Mr. Barn forth was re-elected people's warden. Other officers elected were lay delegates, Mr. W. Kav and Mr. Peter Edgar; sidesmen, Messrs. J. W. Kay, Stanley, Joseph Taylor, J. Cowden, R. Knight, J. J. Hands, J. Taylor, Armstrong, E. Clayton, P. Edgar, Greaves, P. Green, T. S. Steel, P. 'Ashford, John Taylor, and James Reddish; auditors, Messrs. Webster and Steel.</p>
        <p>TIIE EDUCATION 'BILL. RESOLUTION OF PROTEST. The Vicar said he thought they ought to express an opi,don with regard to the Education Bill. He did not look upon the Bill as an Education Bill at all as there was nothing constructive about it, but thought it might be described as a.nonconformist attack upon the Church of England. He hardly thought he would have—to defend himself from a charge of having an extreme man but in regard to the Bill he felt extremely and felt that the proposed measure was absolutely unjust and unfair to the Church of England, in proposing to sweep away trust deeds and in containing the principles of confisca- tion. When they demonstrated that to the general ' public he trusted to their sense of fair play and justice to sweep away any Government which adopted such a measure. In regard to religious instruction the Bill was absolutely unfair to the Church of England, whilst it actually endowed Noncon- formity, as the undenomivational instruction which WQuld be given was their religion. He was sorry for anxone, from a moral standpoint, who could regard the Bill as fair or as an honest attempt to deal with the question. (Hear, bear.) Mr. Bainforth moved a resolution of protest. Mr. Edgar secoifded, and Mr. Steel supported the resolution which was heartily approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>STRETTO.</head>
        <p>STRETTO.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting of St. Matthew's Church was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The Vicar pre- sided over a very fair attendance. The churchwardens' accounts were submitted and passed. The debt of the church had been reduced by £7 but their was still an adverse balance of £3O. The retiring wardens were re-elected for the ensuing year, viz., Mr. 0. W. Willett by the Vicar and Mr. George Blackshaw as people's warden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-04-21_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>STOCK AND SUARE MARKETS.</head>
        <p>STOCK AND SUARE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>The annual congregational tea party and concert in connection with Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church took place on Monday evening and proved highly successful. About 100 sat down to a capital tea the after-proceed- ings were presided over by Mr. James Dodds. The financial report was presented by Mr, Wadsworth, the Sunday School report by Mr. J. Gorst, and the Wesley Guild report by Mr. E. G. Rickers. An interesting musical programme was ,gena through, including a duet by the Misses Johnson and Moore, songs by Miss Hardy and Miss Johnson, and ajpianoforte:solo by Mr. E. G. Rickers. During the evening short addresses were delivered by the chairman, and the Revs. S. Pitt and A. C. Parker. Upwards of £lOO were promised towards the extension and alteration of the chapel. Refresh- ments were served and a pleasaat gathering closed with the singing of the Doxology. The Rev. D. R. James' Departure. This week the Rev. D. R. James resigns the curacy o St. Peter's Church to take up a similar position at the Parish Church, Wellington. He will be succeeded at St. Peter's by the Rev. Henry Russell, Blaenanon, Mon- mouthshire. During the four years he has been in Warrington the Rev. D. R. James has earned the affection of the parishioners of St. Peter's and on Sunday evening when he preached his farewell sermon there was a large congregation.—At a special meeting in the afternoon Mr. T. Steel, on behalf of those attending the services, presented Mr. James with a richly embroidered stole, and, in doing so, said that during the four years and a half he had been connected with it Mr. James had done his duty to the best of his ability, and the members of the men's services appre- ciated his work. He hoped the stole would be a reminder of the happy meetings they had attended in that parish. The Rev. D. R. James suitably replied.— A meeting of the members of the congregation under the chairmanship of the Vicar was held in the school, John-street, on Monday night, to bid farewell to Mr. James.—The Vicar wished Mr. James every happiness and success in his new sphere of labour. He felt sure ho was voicing the feelings of the parish in that regard. (Applause.)—Alderman Roberts, who spoke in high terms of Mr. James's work, presented to him a handsome dressing bag, which bore the following inscription :—" St. Peter's Church. Presented to the Rev. D. R. James by members of the congregation as a token of esteem and regard. Warrington, April Nth, 1900." (Applause.)—Mr. James, in reply, said when he came among them, over four years ago, he did not come as an inexperienced man. Ile had been ten years a Congregational minister previous to that. He entered the Church of England not for money; indeed he had left one of the wealthiest denominations in England, and he was now actually receiving less money than before. It was not money for which he came into the Church, but principle. (Applause.) He could assure them that he was not taking this step without careful and prayerful consideration, and he would carry away with him a happy remembrance of their many kind- nesses to him. He had much pleasure in accepting that token of their regard, and thanked them all, from the Vicar downwards,for their kindness to him. (Applause.)</p>
